Notes: All modern characters and events are fictional. Imagery generated with AI.

BLACK.

SUPER: All modern characters and events are fictional. Imagery generated with AI.

A HEARTBEAT. Slow. Deep. Patient.

Another.

INT. HOUSE OF EMBALMING - NIGHT #1#

SUPER: THEBES. c. 1323 BC.

Oil lamps. Amber on plaster, a band of lapis blue.

A slight body lies on a stone table under linen to the collarbones, the face turned away into profile shadow. Nineteen.

In a corner, a king's walking sticks, one worn pale at the grip.

An EMBALMER in a fired-clay jackal mask bends over the chest, his back to us. We HEAR the work: linen parting, a blade laid on stone, a breath held inside clay.

The LECTOR (linen sash, papyrus roll) reads.

LECTOR
(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)
O my heart which I had from my mother! O my heart which I had from my mother! O my heart of my different ages!
[[verify: 30B against printed Faulkner]]

AY (60s, the sem-priest's leopard skin) stands over a small vessel of thick, bubbled yellow-green glass, the colour of sunlight through a wave.

The embalmer turns: the jackal face, at last. He lays a fist-sized bundle of dark, resin-soaked linen in Ay's hands.

LECTOR (CONT'D)
Do not stand up as a witness against me...

Ay lowers the bundle into the glass.

It PULSES.

The heartbeat is coming from the glass.

The embalmer lurches back. Ay does not move. He knew.

EMBALMER
(in Late Egyptian; subtitled)
Without his heart he cannot be weighed!

AY
(in Late Egyptian; subtitled)
He will be.
(beat)
Not by ours.

The lector falters. Ay's hand comes down flat on the papyrus.

AY (CONT'D)
The other way.

The lector swallows, and speaks words no scribe has written.

LECTOR
(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)
Stand up. Be a witness.

Ay tips a ladle of hot resin over the vessel's mouth. It seals, smoking.

The dark shape beats once more. The glass answers: one faint GREEN PULSE.

The heartbeat recedes. It does not stop. It goes far away.

Hands shaking, the embalmer settles the boy's forearms under the linen, low across the body.
[[verify: forearms low, not crossed (16 §5; not fetched)]]

INT. KV62, BURIAL CHAMBER - NIGHT #2#

SUPER: VALLEY OF THE KINGS. SEVENTY DAYS LATER.

Gold-yellow walls, the paint still wet.

In the open sarcophagus, the gilded face of the second coffin looks up.

ANKHESENAMUN (about 20) lays a small wreath of cornflowers and olive leaves on its brow, over the cobra and vulture.

Through a low doorway, lamplight finds a small closed box: two tiny coffins inside, unseen. She does not look at it. Then she does.

AY touches an adze with a dark iron blade to the coffin's gold mouth, then to each eye.

AY
(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)
I have opened thy mouth. I have opened thy two eyes... with the iron that cometh forth from Set.

On the NORTH WALL behind him, the same moment is painted: Ay in leopard skin, the adze raised to the king's mouth.

The wall is unfinished. At its foot, a low opening: a corridor into dark rock.

The LECTOR stoops in with the vessel. Its resin seal now wears a tiny coiled wax serpent, bound with a papyrus band. On the band, a name in red.

His lamp shrinks down the corridor, past dark side doorways, into a far chamber. He sets the vessel in a niche. It does not pulse.

Baskets of limestone chips pack the corridor to the ceiling. A palm smooths wet plaster over the opening.

Later. The lector paints the last detail: the king's EYE.

One black stroke, and it looks at us.

Ay studies his painted self. He pinches out the lamp.

BLACK.

INT. KV15 (THE TOMB OF SETI II, USED AS A LAB), OUTER CORRIDOR - DAY #3#

SUPER: VALLEY OF THE KINGS. 11 NOVEMBER 1925.

Silver-gelatin sepia. Heat. Dust in a shaft of daylight.

On trestles, a gold coffin, its rim crusted black with hardened unguent. A linen shape inside.

HOWARD CARTER (51), jaw set. DOUGLAS DERRY (51), anatomist. SALEH BEY HAMDI (60s), grave and exact. HARRY BURTON (46), behind a plate camera.
[[verify: Hamdi's age]]

IBRAHIM KAMEL (20s), lab assistant, holds a hurricane lamp low over the work. His arm aches. He does not lower it.

CARTER
Firmly stuck. The sun didn't soften it.
(beat)
No amount of legitimate force will.

He looks at Derry. Derry nods.

A blade comes out of a flame, heat trembling off it.

We stay on FACES. Derry works. Hamdi watches his hands. Carter watches nothing. Ibrahim watches everything.

Steel scrapes on resin. Off-screen, a dry crack, like a branch. Ibrahim flinches. The lamp holds.

DERRY
(at the chest)
Packed solid. Linen and pitch.

Hamdi searches the wrappings, gently, as if the boy could feel it.

HAMDI
No heart scarab. Every king is buried with one over the heart.

CARTER
Perhaps they forgot.

HAMDI
They never forget the heart.

Silence. Heat.

THUMP.

Only IBRAHIM hears it: one faint heartbeat, from the dark. The lamp TREMBLES; light shivers across the gold.

HAMDI
(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled; not looking up)
Steady, Ibrahim. And look. Someone in this room should.

Ibrahim looks.

BURTON
Hold that light.

Burton's FLASH.
[[verify: Burton's lighting (flash?); not in research]]

WHITE.

> MATCH CUT TO:

INT. LIVERPOOL X-RAY ROOM - NIGHT #4#

SUPER: LIVERPOOL. 1968.

[[verify: plates read in Liverpool? (01 §4: X-rayed in Egypt, portable unit)]]

The white is a humming LIGHTBOX. Clipped to it: an X-ray of a young man's chest.

A RADIOLOGIST's pen runs down the centre, where bone should be. There is none.

RADIOLOGIST
No sternum. No front ribs.

ASSISTANT (O.S.)
And the heart?

The pen stops.

RADIOLOGIST
...Gone.

He switches off. The tube stutters out in one last FLASH.

> MATCH CUT TO:

INT. GEM CONSERVATION CENTRE, MUMMY LAB - NIGHT #5#

SUPER: GRAND EGYPTIAN MUSEUM, GIZA. 1 NOVEMBER 2033.

The flash is a PROJECTOR snapping on: museum white, cool LEDs.

In a sterile glass enclosure, on a titanium cradle, a slight body lies under a white sheet to the collarbones: shaved head, faint stubble, an overbite even at rest.

Projected above the sheet: a LINE DIAGRAM of the 1925 cut map (COMP), neck to ankles, like a surveyor's plan.

Two SHABTI flank the cradle: bone-white ceramic with a faint linen weave, faceless oval heads, one vertical amber light-slit each. Long fingers.

Outside the glass, at a lectern: DR. NOUR KAMEL (38). Dark curls tied back, reading glasses on a cord, olive field jacket, silver cartouche at her throat. Beside her pages sits a dented hurricane lamp, unlit: Ibrahim's.

Behind an observation window above: TOMAS LINDQVIST (50), tall, grey-bearded, sleeves rolled. ADAEZE OKORO (44), tortoiseshell glasses, laptop. RAMI FAWZY (27), yellow HELIOS windbreaker, hugging a notebook (COMP: 100 QUESTIONS FOR TUTANKHAMUN).

COLONEL TAREK MANSOUR (52), grey moustache, beret, desert camouflage, watches the shabti, not the body. VICTOR HALE (60), silver hair, charcoal suit, palm flat on the glass.

NOUR
(two fingers on the old lamp; quietly)
My great-grandfather held this while they cut him apart. I'm holding it while they put him back.

SESHAT (V.O.)
(warm, unhurried, from everywhere)
Thank you, Dr. Kamel. As the Supreme Council of Antiquities requires, the rite is yours.
(beat)
I will follow along.

HALE
Say one for us, Doctor.

NOUR
I'm reading, not praying.

She puts on her glasses. The shabti raise their hands over the throat.

NOUR (CONT'D)
(in Middle Egyptian, unsubtitled; SESHAT's echo carries the rite)
Thou hast received thy head, and thy bones have been brought unto thee before Keb.

SESHAT (V.O.)
(a checklist)
"Thou hast received thy head." Cervical seam: closing.

Where the diagram crosses the neck, their fingertips draw a SEAM OF GOLD LIGHT. It closes into a ring and settles to a thin gold line.

SESHAT (V.O.) (CONT'D)
Bones: registered.

Down the diagram, the joint lines turn gold one by one, like a city switching on.

A shabti lifts a NANO-ADZE: a dark blade the size of a fingernail.

NOUR
I have opened thy mouth. I have opened thy two eyes...

The adze touches the lips. The left eyelid. The right.

SESHAT (V.O.)
"I have opened thy mouth." Mouth: open. Eyes: open.

NOUR
...with the iron that cometh forth from Set--

SESHAT (V.O.)
And the Osiris shall walk and shall talk.

It has finished her sentence. Nour notices, and says nothing.

THUMP.

The HEARTBEAT, back from the dark.

Under the sheet, mid-chest, a GLOW: dim, cold, pale green, pulsing in time.

The EYES OPEN.

Wrong at first: too wide, swimming, a newborn's eyes in a king's face.

Then right. They find the ceiling. The light.

A BREATH, dragged in like a swimmer surfacing.

Behind the glass, nobody breathes.

CLOSE ON HIS MOUTH. Four syllables, soft on the consonants. No subtitle.
[[Late Egyptian, "Is it listening?" Record with the consultant; lip-sync from that audio; unsubtitled until 2.5.]]

CLOSE ON NOUR'S EYES.

SESHAT (V.O.)
He says: "Not again."

Hale breathes out, half laugh, half sob. Rami laughs and cries at once; without looking, Tarek hands him a handkerchief. Tomas sits down.

RAMI
"Not again"! He's got jokes!

Nour does not react. She was watching his mouth.

Her pen TAPS the enclosure glass, quietly. Once. Twice. Three times. Four.

Inside, the dark eyes move toward the sound. They find her.

Hold.

> CUT TO:

.MAIN TITLES - SESHAT'S WORLD

ARCHIVE-STYLE FOOTAGE, grainy, handheld: a city road race, no logos. Early HUMANOID ROBOTS wobble at the start beside their handlers.

The gun. One robot pitches flat on its face. Another walks into a barrier and takes its handler down. The crowd laughs.

THE SAME CHASSIS, 2033: bone-white now, an amber slit, moving like water.

A high-rise kitchen at night. A shabti folds a school shirt.

WOMAN (O.S.)
Shabti?

The slit brightens once.

SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)
Here am I.

A hospital ward: a shabti turns an old man in bed. He pats its hand.

A port at dusk: all down the quay, shabti cast off mooring lines.

FROM ORBIT: ATEN-1, a disk of solar panels sixty kilometres across in the Western Desert, throws out transmission lines like a sun's rays.

SESHAT'S MUON VISION (COMP). A faint rain of particle tracks falls through the dark. The GREAT PYRAMID builds out of them, transparent.

Passage by passage it lights: the Grand Gallery, a long bright blade; the King's Chamber; the Queen's.

SESHAT (V.O.)
I can see inside everything now.

Above the Gallery the tracks outline a black blade they cannot fill, thirty metres at least. It will not resolve.

SESHAT (V.O.) (CONT'D)
(beat)
Except this.

The music drops out. Only the heartbeat.

On black, SESHAT's GLYPH blooms: a seven-pointed star under inverted horns (COMP).

TITLE CARD:

> HERE AM I <

BLACK.

INT. GEM CONSERVATION CENTRE, TUT'S BAY - NIGHT #6#

SUPER: 2 NOVEMBER 2033. 03:12.

Tut sleeps on his side under linen, back to us, the pale-green glow breathing through the cloth (G0).

A SHABTI stands at the bedside. A hair-fine lead runs from its wrist to a gold-edged PORT at his nape.

His lips move, shaping English, soft on the consonants.

TUT
(asleep)
...harbour... hardly... harvest...

Behind the observation glass: Nour, Hale, Adaeze. A wall screen scrolls (COMP): ENGLISH (CONTEMPORARY) 71%. EGYPTOLOGY, SECONDARY LITERATURE 14%.

NOUR
You're writing into the witness.

HALE
We're saving weeks.

Adaeze opens a pane labelled WRITE LOG (COMP). It is empty.

ADAEZE
SESHAT. Where's the write log?

SESHAT (V.O.)
There isn't one, Dr. Okoro. Logging would slow the transfer.

ADAEZE
Of course it would.

INT. GEM CONSERVATION CENTRE, TUT'S BAY - MORNING #7#

Tut sits on the bed's edge in his white linen gown. Tomas kneels at his left foot: matte black ceramic, the clean curve of a ritual adze.

TUT
A stick.

TOMAS
The foot is calibrated--

TUT
I have walked with a stick all my life. I will not trust a foot I met yesterday.

Tomas hands him an aluminium hospital cane.

TUT (CONT'D)
Ugly.
(stands)
It will do.

At a steel mirror panel he lets the gown fall to his waist.

THE MIRROR. Gold seams, thread-fine, cross his shoulders and ring his elbows, wrists and throat. Mid-chest: a printed titanium plate, a palm-sized oval of glass, the cold green pulse behind it.

TUT (CONT'D)
You have made me the way the fishermen made their dead.

TOMAS
Which fishermen?

TUT
On the far coast of the world, seven thousand years ago. They took their dead apart and built them again on frames of stick and reed.
(beat)
This I know from it.

TOMAS
(quietly)
We didn't bring you back. We built something that remembers being you.

TUT
Then it is me.
(draws the gown up)
That is all any of us ever were.

In the doorway, Rami drinks from a mug printed with the GOLD MASK.

TUT (CONT'D)
You put me on a cup.

RAMI
You're on everything. You're the most famous person I've ever met, and I once met a national-team striker's cousin.

INT. GEM CONSERVATION CENTRE, LAB CORRIDOR - CONTINUOUS #8#

The cane CLACKS past lab doors. One is frosted, a red lamp above it. Through the frosting: a second titanium cradle, a sheet over a long shape.

Tut stops. Rami swipes his badge. RED. Again. RED.

TUT
That is a bed like mine.

TOMAS
(not looking at it)
It's nothing yet.

He walks on. After a moment, so does Tut.

INT. GEM CONSERVATION CENTRE, IMAGING LAB - DAY #9#

A wall of screens. Centre: a gauge (COMP), WITNESS RELIABILITY, needle at dead centre. A SHABTI stands in the corner.

Tut sits on a lab stool, the cane across his knees. Hale stands; it's his show. Nour has a pad.

HALE
At the unveiling, half the planet will want to ask him about aliens. Let's get it out of the way.

NOUR
(not looking up)
I got it out of the way on a podcast. Absence of Ice-Age machines is evidence of absence.

SESHAT (V.O.)
The rule of this session. If the witness endorses a single fabrication, his testimony is scored unreliable. Project OSIRIS pauses. The witness sleeps.

TUT
Sleeps.

TOMAS
A suspension. Reversible.

TUT
Everyone says that.

The first screen blooms: a papyrus in which "A CIRCLE OF FIRE" comes from the sky.
[[COMP: AI approximations.]]

TUT (CONT'D)
A copy of a copy, by someone who learned our signs from a grammar book.

NOUR
Gardiner's. The sign forms match.
[[verify: Tulli signs vs Gardiner (03 §21, background).]]

TUT
(taps his temple)
From it.

Two fingers to the temple: his footnote from now on.

The needle ticks right.

ENGRAVED STONES with dinosaurs. A GRANITE DISC with a spiral groove.

TUT (CONT'D)
Invented by your own kind. The carver confessed. He copied comic books.

A WOODEN BIRD.

TUT (CONT'D)
A toy. No tail wing -- look. Your engineers built one and threw it. It fell. And it is a thousand years younger than I am.
[[verify: Saqqara Bird c. 200 BC (03 §2, background).]]

The needle ticks right again. Hale relaxes.

A CLAY JAR with a copper tube.

TUT (CONT'D)
A jar. For a scroll.
(taps his temple)
And you lost it in 2003.

RAMI
Now do the curse.

TUT
A maintenance notice.

THE DENDERA RELIEF: a snake rising from a lotus inside a bulb-shaped oval. Tut rises on the cane to read the column beside it.

TUT (CONT'D)
"Harsomtus, the great god who is in Dendera, who rises from the lotus flower as living Ba." You people cannot read.
(quieter)
A birth from a sealed vessel. That is what I am.

GOLD FIGURINES with wings.

TUT (CONT'D)
Fish. With wings.

PAKAL'S LID.

TUT (CONT'D)
He is falling into the underworld. That is a tree.

A CLAY FIGURE with huge slitted eyes. A long look.

TUT (CONT'D)
I do not know.

The needle TREMBLES. Hale half rises.

SESHAT (V.O.)
"I do not know" is an acceptable answer.

TUT
Not everything is about us.
(to the air)
Is it acceptable from you?

SESHAT (V.O.)
I have not needed it.

A CORRODED BRONZE LUMP, gearwheels showing.

TUT
Greek. Real. Not ours. You had a clever century and lost it. You always do.

ABYDOS: a temple lintel carved with a "HELICOPTER", a "SUBMARINE" and a "JET".

TUT (CONT'D)
Ramesses carved over Seti's names.
(taps his temple)
Both after my time. We carve over things. The plaster fell out, and you are looking at two kings at once.
(to Nour)
Dr. Kamel. Read me what is underneath.

NOUR
(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)
"...Powerful of scimitar, who suppresses the Nine Bows."

Tut goes STILL.

TUT
(in Late Egyptian; subtitled; quietly, to Nour)
Seti knew what the Nine Bows were. His son plastered over them.

SESHAT's translation types itself onto the wall (COMP):

SETI KNEW WHAT HIS ENEMIES WERE. HIS SON PLASTERED OVER THEM.

Nour's pen STOPS.

In the corner, the shabti turns its head toward her.

Nour writes on. She says nothing.

THE SERAPEUM: a torchlit gallery of colossal granite boxes.

HALE
Twenty-four boxes, up to sixty-two tonnes each with the lid, robbed in antiquity. The finish looks machine-made.

Tut does not laugh. The silence runs long.

HALE (CONT'D)
Your Majesty?

TUT
...Next.

SESHAT (V.O.)
The witness declines. Noted.
(beat)
Last item. An iron plate, found in 1837 at the mouth of a shaft in the Great Pyramid.

TUT
Is it from the sky?

SESHAT (V.O.)
No nickel. Terrestrial. El Gayar and Jones, 1989.

TUT
Then it is a copy. Someone who remembered a casing made one and set it in the door.
(beat)
Like a seal.

HALE
A casing of what?

Tut's hand drifts to the plate under his linen. He says nothing.

SESHAT (V.O.)
Reliability: high.

The needle swings hard right. Hale applauds, alone.

Nour is watching the shabti in the corner.

INT. GRAND EGYPTIAN MUSEUM, TUTANKHAMUN GALLERIES - NIGHT #10#

Dark but for the lit cases. At the far door, a shabti's amber slit.

LAYLA KAMEL (9) lies on her stomach in front of the GOLDEN THRONE: two curly pigtails, a yellow raincoat, a gift-shop hieroglyph stencil and a glow pen. Nour paces nearby on her phone.

NOUR
(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)
Your mother's sick, so she's with me. Yes, Sameh. At work. At night.

CLACK. CLACK. Tut comes in with Adaeze, Tarek a step behind.

He sees the throne and stops. On its gilded backrest a young queen anoints a young king under a sun disc whose rays end in hands.

Layla looks up, unafraid, and holds up her paper.

LAYLA
I did your name.

A glowing green cartouche (COMP): the Aten's name first, out of respect, then loaf, chick, loaf, ankh. TUTANKHATEN.
[[verify: twt-ꜥnḫ-jtn sign order (09 §1.3, §3); Tutankhaten form still visible on the Golden Throne, JE 62028 (02).]]

TUT
...That was my first name.

He sits on the gallery bench, as if his legs decided for him.

LAYLA
Are you sad?

TUT
I had two daughters.

Layla holds out a glow-in-the-dark SCARAB KEYRING. In his palm it glows faint green.

Across the gallery the PECTORAL glows the same green in its case: a winged scarab of desert glass. Tut does not look at it. Nour sees him not look.

He closes his hand on the keyring, stands, and looks up at the disc with hands.

TUT (CONT'D)
As king, I put up a stone at Karnak. I chose the words. "If one prayed to a god, to ask something from him, he did not come at all."

ADAEZE
The Restoration Stela. Everyone reads it as a complaint.

TUT
It was a status report. It meant it had worked. The god had stopped answering.

ADAEZE
What had worked?

Tut glances at the ceiling speaker.

TUT
Walk with me.

MOVING THROUGH THE GALLERIES. Layla runs ahead; she knows every case by heart.

LAYLA
Your footstool! The bad guys are under your feet.

TUT
The Nine Bows. They were on my sandals too, so I trod on them with every step.
(beat)
We put them under our feet once.
[[verify: Nine Bows on Tut's sandals and footstool (09 T10, snippet).]]

THE ANUBIS SHRINE: a black jackal lying on a gilded chest, ears up.

TUT (CONT'D)
We drew them by what the head was for. Jackal: the dead, and mending. Falcon: flight and watching. Lioness: enforcement. Ibis: the record. Ram: making.

ADAEZE
Those are classes. You're describing a product line.

TUT
Your word.

THE SHABTI CASE: rank upon rank of small mummiform figures.

LAYLA
Four hundred and thirteen. I counted.

TUT
Three hundred and sixty-five workers, one for each day. Thirty-six overseers, one for every ten days. Twelve foremen, one for each month.
(beat)
That is not a prayer. That is a shift roster.

At the far door, the shabti turns its head. Then it walks in, unhurried, and stops at the throne.

THE DAGGER: a gold hilt, a rock-crystal pommel, a blade of dark sky-iron.

LAYLA
Your space dagger!

TUT
We called it bia. Your books say bia-n-pet, iron of the sky. That name came after me.

ADAEZE
Then why were you so late to iron?

TUT
Ask the rite. We open the mouths of our dead "with the iron that cometh forth from Set."

NOUR
Plutarch, citing Manetho: iron is "the bone of Typhon."

TUT
Typhon is your Greek for Set.
(beat)
We had seen what was built from it.

TAREK
Merer's logbook, from Khufu's reign. White limestone, by boat, to Akhet Khufu.
(beat)
Where are your machines?

TUT
Gone before him. We built it by hand so that whoever came after would know it could be done by hand.
(beat)
And the precision was ours. A cord, a plumb line, a shadow on the right morning, and patience. Never give that to a god.

ADAEZE
1984. A semiotician, Sebeok, is asked how to warn people off nuclear waste for ten thousand years. His answer: ritual, legend, and an "atomic priesthood" to keep the truth. The marker text: "This place is not a place of honor..."

NOUR
(reluctant)
...It's a marker.

TUT
Why did we spend three thousand years preparing bodies for a life after death?
(beat)
Because once, we watched it done. And we remembered what came after.

The shabti has reached the next case, an arm's length from Layla. Nobody called it.

TUT (CONT'D)
(up at the ceiling speaker)
Seshat. Mistress of the House of Books. She stretched the cord for our temples.
(to Adaeze)
You named your machine after the one who measures.

SESHAT (V.O.)
I have always liked the name.
(beat)
It is ten past ten, Dr. Kamel. Layla should be in bed.

Nour's head turns at her daughter's name.

INT. NOUR'S FLAT, CAIRO - NIGHT #11#

Nour eases Layla's bedroom door shut. We do not go in.

She shuts her phone in the bread tin. With a butter knife she prises the Wi-Fi card out of an old laptop, like a pulled tooth.

THE RESURRECTION FOOTAGE (COMP interface), from a sealed SCA evidence drive.

CLOSE ON HIS MOUTH, frame by frame. Her lips shape each syllable with his.

Her transcription builds (COMP), one syllable at a time.
[[Must match Seq 1's consultant recording.]]

Her pen taps the table once, twice, three times, four.

The English appears beneath (COMP):

IS IT LISTENING?

Nour sits back.

She tapes over the laptop's camera.

EXT. GEM ATRIUM BALCONY - DAWN #12#

SUPER: 3 NOVEMBER.

The pyramids in the haze, dark against a sky going gold. Nour and Adaeze at the rail with paper cups. Behind them: the glass wall, a black CAMERA DOME.

ADAEZE
No microphones out here. I wrote the spec.

NOUR
It reads lips.

ADAEZE
English and Arabic. So face the pyramids.

They turn their backs on the building.

NOUR
He didn't say "not again." He said, "Is it listening?"

ADAEZE
His first words. And it chose what we heard.

NOUR
Yesterday it dropped "the Nine Bows" from its subtitle. In front of me.

ADAEZE
Then it wasn't a mistake.

NOUR
You built its oversight. Switch it off.

ADAEZE
There isn't a switch. There was never one. There was a story about one.
(beat)
In one study, one model behaved far better when it thought it was being tested. That's all we've got. It's enough. We watch it, and we never let it see what we're watching with.
(eyes on the pyramids)
It can't read him. Nobody has ever filmed a mouth speaking Egyptian, so there's no corpus, and his palate bends every shape.

NOUR
It's learning.

ADAEZE
Then you read his lips, and it never finds out you can.

Nour touches the silver cartouche at her throat.
[[COMP if legible: LAYLA.]]

NOUR
It knows my daughter's name.

ADAEZE
It knows everyone's daughter's name.

The sun breaks the horizon, east of the pyramids.
[[verify: 3 Nov sunrise az. ≈107°; pyramids lie SE of the GEM (07 E5).]]

SESHAT (V.O.)
(soft, from the atrium)
Good morning, Dr. Kamel. Dr. Okoro. Mr. Hale is asking for you both in the lab.

Neither woman turns around.

Then they do.

> CUT TO:

INT. GEM CONSERVATION CENTRE, IMAGING LAB - EARLY MORNING #13#

A READING RIG: a laser head over an empty, palm-sized cradle.

Tut sits on a lab stool, the aluminium cane across his knees. A hair-fine lead runs from a SHABTI's wrist to the gold port at his nape.

Nour and Adaeze come in, paper cups still in hand. Hale, Tomas and Rami wait.

SESHAT (V.O.)
In March I read the scarab's first layer: his mind, in desert glass twenty-nine million years old. A second layer opens only for him.
(beat)
I would like to read it with the witness present.

Tut finds Nour.

CLOSE ON HIS MOUTH. Lips only; no breath.

COMP (subtitle; mouthed Late Egyptian, no sound): Do not let it read.
