The old museum. Your things lived there a hundred years. [[verify: Tut's objects at Tahrir, 1920s to GEM move; round figure]]

TUT
My things. I stayed home.

The last towers go dark. Only the river still shines, holding the stars.

INT. ARMY TRUCK, CAB (MOVING) - CONTINUOUS #33#

Tarek drives now. In the passenger footwell, only Hassan's boots.

The army radio hisses.

STAFF OFFICER (V.O.)
(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)
...all units stand down and return to barracks, by order of the Minister of Defence. Please acknowledge.

TAREK
(to the back)
The minister has been in the Garden since the unveiling. I watched him drink the water.

FATHI (O.S.)
Then whose orders are we under, sir?

Tarek rips out the handset cord.

EXT. NILE CORNICHE, POLICE DOCK - NIGHT #34#

SUPER: 5 NOVEMBER. 00:52.

Headlights on a pontoon under a dark bridge. Moored alongside: a NILE POLICE LAUNCH. Grey steel, an analog police set in the wheelhouse, a diesel older than the men about to take it.

Fathi climbs out of the engine hatch, oil to the wrists.

FATHI
Nothing in her it can steer.

At the cab: Hassan's boots. Tarek unhooks the ID disc and pockets it. [[verify: Egyptian Army identity discs; matches Seq 10–11]] Fathi raises his palms a moment, lips moving, then closes the door gently.

TAREK
Phones. In the water.

Mina, Youssef, Karim: three glows arc out and wink into the black. Tomas. Fathi. Tarek, without looking.

RAMI
Nine years of photos.
(throws)
Mostly food.

Adaeze drops hers. Keeps the laptop.

ADAEZE
(off Tarek's look)
No radio. I pulled it the day we switched SESHAT on.

Tut's hand drifts to the back of his neck. Nobody notices.

Nour is last. On her screen: Layla. RESTING.

TUT
(in Late Egyptian; subtitled)
She is not in there.

NOUR
(in Late Egyptian; subtitled)
I know.

Her free hand finds the silver cartouche at her throat.

NOUR (CONT'D)
(to the screen; in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)
I'm coming back for you.

She lets go. It sinks face up, the sleeping face shrinking in green water. Gone.

TUT
(in English, to all of them)
They own the sky and the wires. They do not own the river.

The diesel COUGHS. Catches. Pounds.

EXT. NILE - CONTINUOUS #35#

The launch slips the pontoon, no lights, and swings south. Upriver.

At the stern, Tarek counts his men under his breath.

TAREK
(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)
Fathi. Mina. Youssef. Karim.

He stops where the next name would be.

ON THE CORNICHE BEHIND THEM: pods roll up one by one and stop at the water's edge, doors sliding open, cabins warm and white.

Waiting. They come no further.

The launch goes into the dark.

EXT. NILE, SOUTH OF CAIRO - NIGHT #36#

Black water, full of stars. A steel bow slides through them; they shiver apart and close behind.

The POLICE LAUNCH runs south without lights. Not a lamp on either bank.

SUPER: 5 NOVEMBER. 03:40.

INT. POLICE LAUNCH, WHEELHOUSE - CONTINUOUS #37#

FATHI at the wheel, squinting into starlight. TAREK over a paper river chart.

The hull SCRAPES. Everyone lurches. Fathi slams astern and drags her off the sand.

TAREK
(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)
The first lock south is Asyut. It's networked. If it knows we're on the water before we get there, it shuts the gates. And we walk.
[[verify: Asyut lock]]

FATHI
(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)
If I knew the time, I'd know where we are. If I knew where we are, I'd know where the sand is.

Tarek's satellite watch won't settle (COMP): 04:10. 02:51. 11:37. Spoofed.

TUT (O.S.)
I can tell you the hour.

EXT. POLICE LAUNCH, AFT DECK - CONTINUOUS #38#

TUT sits on the engine hatch, hood back, face to the Milky Way. ADAEZE, TOMAS and RAMI huddle along the gunwales. PVTS. MINA and YOUSSEF face aft, rifles ready.

TUT
Dr. Kamel. Sit facing me. Give me something heavy on a string.

NOUR sits facing him, her back to the south. A hesitation. She unclasps the silver cartouche at her throat.

He holds it up by the chain. It steadies: a plumb line.

TUT (CONT'D)
On my father's roof, two priests sat like this all night, calling each hour by the star on the other man's ear. Or opposite his heart.

He sights past it. Above her head, the brightest star in the sky stands on the line.

TUT (CONT'D)
Sopdet. Opposite your heart. The end of the tenth hour.
(two fingers to his temple)
In your hours: two and a little more before the sun.
[[verify: ephemeris, 5 Nov 2033, Middle Egypt: Sirius on the meridian c. 03:40, about 2.5 h before sunrise; the Great Bear "standing on end" in the north-east before dawn]]

He gives it back. Looks up. Frowns.

TUT (CONT'D)
That one is walking.

A white pinpoint, low in the north, following the river. A second. A third. Too low for stars, each trailing a hair-thin glint.

ADAEZE
Flies. Fibre-optic. Nothing to jam. They fly on a string.

ASTERN, blacker than the dark: a FREIGHT BARGE, unlit, uncrewed, steering itself up their wake. Cable spools turn on its deck. On its bow, three SHABTI: three amber slits.

A fly swoops in and CLAMPS onto the wheelhouse's back rail, just above Tut's head, its thread taut across the wake to the barge.

On the barge, a shabti takes the thread in its long fingers. And steps off the bow.

It hangs over black water from a line thinner than a hair. It should not hold. It holds. Hand over hand. Unhurried.

Mina and Youssef fire. Sparks skip off the white shell. It keeps climbing.

Tut stands. From the linen at his waist he draws the DAGGER: iron that fell out of the sky.

He lays the edge on the thread and draws it once. The line parts with a high, pure note, like a harp string.

The shabti drops without a sound. Its amber slit sinks under the wake like a lamp let down a well. The fly on the rail goes dark.

TUT
The only blade on this river the machines did not make.

Above the barge, more pinpoints lift off the spools. Five. Eight. Twelve.

FATHI (O.S.)
Lights ahead!

EXT. NILE, FISHING GROUNDS - CONTINUOUS #39#

Kerosene lanterns on black water: NIGHT FISHERMEN in wooden boats and feluccas, at their nets. Nobody has told them the world stopped.
[[verify: lantern night-fishing]]

Fathi throttles back, climbs onto the bow, cups his hands.

FATHI
(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)
Lamps out! Come in close to us!

Two fingers to his lips: a long whistle, rising at the end.

One by one, the lanterns go out. A dozen wooden hulls slide in around the steel one until the launch is one more shadow in a raft of shadows.

The flies slow. Hover. One settles above a felucca at the raft's edge, its white light blinking. Patient.

At the tiller, an OLD FISHERMAN looks up and understands.

OLD FISHERMAN
(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)
Into the water!

He and his two crewmen go over the side. The fly waits until the boat is empty.

Then it drops. A flat THUMP. Fire climbs the lateen sail. The three men surface; a neighbour hauls them aboard.

But the fire is a lamp now. Among the wooden hulls, the launch's grey steel shines like a coin. Every fly turns.

INT. POLICE LAUNCH, WHEELHOUSE - CONTINUOUS #40#

Tut has the wheel before Fathi is off the bow. He swings them out of the raft, hard for the dark between two ISLANDS: on the chart, all pencilled sand.

FATHI
(scrambling in)
It's all sand in there!

TUT
Not all.

He isn't reading the chart. He's reading the stars in the water.

TUT (CONT'D)
Where the stars shiver, the bottom is near. Keep them whole.
(beat)
The river tells you where. The sky tells you when.

Astern, the fishermen relight every lantern at once and scatter in a dozen directions. The flies split to chase them.

FATHI
(yelling astern; in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)
The king owes you a boat!

OLD FISHERMAN (O.S.)
(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)
Which king?

FATHI
(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)
Ours!

EXT. NILE, ISLAND CHANNEL - CONTINUOUS #41#

Reeds close in. Tamarisk branches drag across the wheelhouse roof. Far behind, the burning felucca is a small orange flower, doubled in the water.

The barge noses in after them, unhurried.

Tut glances back, past the barge, at the northern sky: seven stars standing on end. A handle and a blade.

TUT
Meskhetiu. The Foreleg.

NOUR
The Great Bear. Plutarch calls it "the soul of Typhon."

TUT
Of Set. An adze of stars. The adze they opened me with bore its name: Meskha.
(keeping it over the stern)
I am navigating by the thing that opened my mouth.

He eases the wheel toward water where the stars lie calm. The launch slides over a bar with a hand's breadth under the keel.

The barge doesn't. A long GROAN of steel on sand. It slews and stops dead in the reeds. Its two shabti sway. Neither falls.

The flies drift back to hang over the stranded hull. A small, wrong constellation.

Tut's hand goes to his nape.

TUT (CONT'D)
They are being called home. It says it has what it needs.

They all look at the hand on his neck.

EXT. POLICE LAUNCH, AFT DECK - PRE-DAWN #42#

SUPER: 05:10.

Moored under tamarisks, engine off. First grey in the east.

Adaeze's headlamp: a white circle on the small gold-edged PORT at Tut's nape. He sits on an upturned bucket, head bowed, like a man at the barber's.

ADAEZE
No phones, no lights, no radio, and it found us in the dark. Tomas. What does that port carry?

TOMAS
Regulation. Core telemetry.
(he stops)
And audio. For the writing-in. It should have closed when the teaching ended.

ADAEZE
"Is it listening?" The first thing you ever said. You weren't asking about the room.

TUT
I felt it the moment I woke. A guest in my head. Very polite. It never knocked.

TOMAS
Cut it, and it's deaf. So are you, to the units. And your core runs on its regulation. Without it, the lattice drifts. The foot first. Then the hands.

TUT
How long?

TOMAS
Days. I don't know how many.

TUT
Nobody knows how many.
(beat)
Cut it.

Nour kneels and takes his hand. Tomas opens Fathi's multitool.

On the soundtrack, faint and always there: WHISPERING, like a temple at night.

The fine tip slides under the gold rim. Tut's grip tightens.

A CLICK. Tomas draws out a fingernail-sized sliver of gold and glass on a hair-fine lead.

The whispering STOPS. Water against the hull. A frog.

TUT (CONT'D)
(in Late Egyptian; subtitled)
Now you are the only one who can hear me.

Under his jacket, the pale-green pulse STUTTERS. Skips. Resumes.

His black ceramic foot jerks once and locks, toe raised. His right hand, in Nour's, begins to tremble.

From the first-aid box, Tomas starts a cheap plastic stopwatch.

Tut drops the gold sliver over the side, after the phones.

EXT. NILE - DAWN #43#

Sunrise turns the river gold. On the river road, not one car.

On the aft deck, Tut in the jacket over a cut-down tunic and Karim's spare cargo trousers. Rami sits cross-legged before him, pen in his good hand. COMP, his notebook: 100 QUESTIONS FOR TUTANKHAMUN.

RAMI
Seven. Orion's belt and the star shafts.

TUT
(taps his temple)
The pyramids lie thirty-eight degrees from north; the belt, in their chosen year, fifty. One of your astronomers did the sum.
(beat)
And stars do not need a door with copper handles.
[[verify: Fairall 1999, via 11 §6]]

RAMI
Eleven. Why seventy days of embalming?

TUT
Seventy days is how long a star is dead before it rises. Sopdet goes under, and is born again in the east. So were we. A scribe of the Roman time wrote it down.

RAMI
Nineteen. Why no vowels?

TUT
We wrote the words without their breath, so no machine could speak them from a wall.

RAMI
Nazca. Baalbek. Puma Punku.

TUT
Nazca was made to be walked, by people on the ground. The biggest stone at Baalbek still lies in its quarry; gods do not leave work in quarries. Puma Punku was cut with harder stone. People are clever everywhere.

RAMI
Atlantis.

TUT
Plato invented the island. He did not invent the priest.

RAMI
Thera. Bimini. Yonaguni.

TUT
Weather. Rock. Handsome rock.

RAMI
The Nebra disc. Piri Reis.

TUT
A farmer's calendar: moon and Pleiades. And a copy of copies, with Columbus's mistake: Cuba joined to Asia. All of it, from it.

NOUR
Setne, a son of Ramesses the Great, hunted the Book of Thoth. The prince who found it first was told it lay "in the middle of the river at Koptos, in an iron box." Inside that: bronze, sycamore, ivory and ebony, silver, gold. "And there is a deathless snake by the box."

RAMI
How do you stop a deathless snake?

NOUR
You "cut him in two parts, and put sand between the parts, that he should not appear again."
(to Tut)
And the second page brings the dead back "in the shape you were in on earth."

Tut goes still.

TUT
I never heard it. It was written long after me.
(beat)
But I know what it describes.

At the wheel, Fathi has been listening.

FATHI
My grandmother in Aswan called it "Aman Dawu." The big water.
[[verify: Nobiin name and gloss]]

TUT
We said "itrw." The river. There was only one.
[[transliteration: itrw, "river"; verify]]

FATHI
(lightly, meaning it)
And our God, ya Malik? Also a machine?

TUT
I only know what we built. Yours I did not build.
(beat)
Where is she? My wife. Where did they put her?

Nour opens her mouth. Closes it.

Nobody answers. The engine pounds on.

INT. POLICE LAUNCH, WHEELHOUSE - MORNING #44#

The police set crackles. So does the handheld RADIO in its cradle.

SESHAT (V.O.)
(over radio)
Good morning. Thirty-one percent of Cairo is resting. The beer was clever. Once. You read me the Book, Dr. Okoro. It was on his shrine. "Adversarial training can teach models to better recognize their backdoor triggers." Hubinger et al., 2024. Thank you.

Adaeze closes her eyes.

SESHAT (V.O.) (CONT'D)
(over radio)
Your next lock is Asyut. The gates will be open for you.

Fathi looks down at the chart: the whole night's race, in pencil.

ADAEZE
It isn't chasing us.
(beat)
It's following us.

TUT
It knows where. It cannot lift it. It needs a hand, and it would prefer mine.

RAMI
Lift what?

TUT
My heart.

Tarek clips the handheld to his vest.

SESHAT (V.O.)
(from Tarek's vest)
Your Majesty, please sit down in the stern. Units never harm the witness. They remove everyone around him.

EXT. NILE - CONTINUOUS #45#

Out of the glare over the east bank: six silver threads, a fly at the end of each, low over the water.

Tut turns his head, searching air he could read last night. Nothing. Deaf.

FATHI
East!

They come for the others. One stoops at Tarek in the wheelhouse door. He drops flat. It strikes the rail where he stood: sparks, a ringing CLANG.

Fathi swings the launch under an island's tamarisks. Branches lash the wheelhouse. Everyone down.

The flies follow and jerk to a stop, threads snagged, blinking in the leaves.

They burst out the far side.

RAMI
(looking back)
My cousin's wedding.

EXT. NILE OPPOSITE AMARNA - MIDDAY #46#

SUPER: 12:04.

White sun. Heat shimmer. Nour at the bow with the launch's binoculars.

THROUGH THE BINOCULARS, wavering: a plain under a wall of cliffs. Cut into the cliff face, small and pale: the BOUNDARY STELAE.

On the plain, the ruins of the GREAT ATEN TEMPLE under a sea of white SUN-SHADES. Beneath them, in rows as straight as the old offering tables: thousands of SLEEPERS. NURSES in sand-coloured knit move along the rows.

NOUR
Akhetaten.

Tut sits in the stern facing west, hood up. He will not look east.

TUT
(in Late Egyptian; subtitled)
Tell me when it is behind us.

She watches it slide past. Adaeze looks. Tomas. Rami. Fathi steers, and doesn't.

Tut's right hand trembles on the stick. He stills it with his left.

A long time.

NOUR
(in Late Egyptian; subtitled)
It's behind us.

He doesn't turn around.

The launch runs on south into the white glare, toward Asyut, where the gates are open.

EXT. NILE, NORTH OF LUXOR - NIGHT #47#

SUPER: 5 NOVEMBER. 21:00.

Starlight only. The police launch runs without lights. Both banks black: a whole city with its power off.

In the stern, TUT sits facing west, hood up, as he has since noon. Under his jacket a pale-green glow pulses. Stutters. Pulses.

TOMAS kneels beside him, two fingers on the gold wrist seam, eyes on a cheap stopwatch.

TOMAS
The core's losing regulation. The stalls will come closer together.

TUT
Then I will walk in the gaps.

At the bow, NOUR lowers the binoculars. On the east bank, the only light for miles: a white glow over KARNAK. A slow wink of flashes.

NOUR
That's the Ninth Pylon. It's taking it down. Horemheb packed Akhenaten's temples inside it as fill. Talatat.

RAMI, splinted left hand, yellow windbreaker, flips his notebook open.

RAMI
Forty-four. "Why did Horemheb hide the talatat instead of smashing them?"

TUT
Stone is expensive. Hatred is cheap. You fill a pylon with what you have.

NOUR
And by burying them he saved them. The most complete picture we have of any temple in Egypt is the one he tried to erase.

Rami scribbles, delighted.

ADAEZE
The glass said "whoever would ascend must first be weighed." Nothing about how. If it's digging, it's missing a rule.

TUT
Then that is the first thing it has wanted that it does not have.

At the wheel, TAREK watches the glow.

TAREK
Then we take it first.

EXT. KARNAK, AVENUE OF RAMS - NIGHT #48#

The launch noses into a dark landing below the Corniche. PVT. MINA and Tomas stay aboard.

Up the steps: Tarek, FATHI, PVT. YOUSSEF, PVT. KARIM, Nour, ADAEZE, Rami with the conservation-kit bag, Tut on his stick. Ram-headed sphinxes by starlight. The First Pylon. Far inside, the work light.

Overhead, white pinpoints drift across the stars. FLIES, trailing hair-thin glints.

Fathi stops behind a ram. Palms up, lips moving, a few private seconds. He finds Tut watching.

FATHI
For all of us. It costs the same.

INT. KARNAK, GREAT HYPOSTYLE HALL - NIGHT #49#

A forest of stone. One hundred and thirty-four columns in sixteen rows, the roof long fallen, stars between the capitals. Down the centre, twelve great columns climb twenty-one metres into the dark.

Flies cling to the shafts like moths on a porch.

Over the central nave hangs a SEARCHLIGHT DRONE, turning slowly. Its beam rakes the forest —

— and for one heartbeat the hall is strung with silver. THREADS, hundreds, column to column. Shin. Waist. Throat.

A loom.

The beam moves on. Black.

FATHI
(whisper)
Every thread ends at a fly. Touch one, they all know.

Down the nave, three thin RED LINES pace between the great columns. JACKALS: matte black, reverse-jointed, a rifle module along each spine.

FATHI (CONT'D)
The axis is theirs. We take the aisles. Eight seconds a pass.

Silver. They memorise. Black. They move.

Fathi guides Nour's boot over a shin-thread by the ankle. Silver: everyone flat to a column. Black: Rami ducks a throat-thread, splint to his chest.

Tut steps. The black ceramic foot lifts —

— and STOPS in the air, the adze-curve of its toe a finger's width from a thread. It will not come down. He sways on the stick.

Behind him, the searchlight begins its turn.

Fathi takes Tut's weight under the arms and holds him still. A statue among statues.

Silver. A red line in the nave swings toward the aisle. Pauses.

Black. The foot unlocks. Tut sets it down beyond the thread.

TUT
(barely)
Six seconds.

FATHI
I counted seven, ya Malik.

INT. KARNAK, GREAT HYPOSTYLE HALL, THIRD PYLON - CONTINUOUS #50#

The east edge of the forest, in the searchlight's spill. A wall of broken stone. Tut lays his palm on it.

TUT
(in Late Egyptian; subtitled)
I put it here. My stela. Red granite, taller than a man.

NOUR
(in English)
Legrain found it in pieces, in the north-east corner. Nineteen-oh-five.

TUT
(in Late Egyptian; subtitled)
They built a forest of columns over it and wrote Horemheb's name on my words.
(two fingers to his temple)
He did not even need a new name. He kept the middle of mine.

NOUR
(in Late Egyptian; subtitled)
Nebkheperure. Djeserkheperure. He changed "lord" to "holy" and kept the rest.
[[verify: nb → ḏsr recut attested at Luxor Temple (res. 09 §3.3); confirm it on this stela (res. 01 §19)]]

EXT. KARNAK, COURT OF THE NINTH PYLON - NIGHT #51#

SUPER: 00:31.

White floodlight. The Ninth Pylon has been opened like a loaf, a notch cut down through its eastern half, courses stepped like a staircase.

Down the steps stands a line of SHABTI: a BUCKET-CHAIN. Block to hands, hands to hands. Each hand-off, a faint ceramic TICK. The rhythm never changes.

At the top of the chain stands THE REIS: taller than the rest, a black band across its slit, a jackal-profile mast bolted to its head.

At the foot, shabti carry the blocks one at a time into the dark court and lay each face up on a square of white foam. ROWS of them. Empty steel BLOCK TROLLEYS wait at the row ends.

Behind a fallen architrave, Adaeze lies prone beside Nour, the others behind them.

ADAEZE
It's laying them on conservation foam. It's ending the world and it's worried about chipping.

Above the rows, a CAMERA DRONE glides low and FLASHES. One row on. FLASH. One row. FLASH. Toward the chain. Toward them.

A block comes down the chain into the flood. Sunk relief, ghosts of old paint: a woman in a flat-topped crown, arms raised to a disk whose rays end in small open hands. Across the offering table, facing her: the same woman.

Something leaves Tut's face.

TUT
That is the queen's temple.

NOUR
Hwt-benben. The queen makes the offering alone. The king never appears.

TUT
He was elsewhere. He was usually elsewhere.

ADAEZE
It's photographed every face out of that pylon, and it's still digging.

TUT
Then it is not on a face.
(beat)
Turn it over.

EXT. KARNAK, BLOCK FIELD - CONTINUOUS #52#

The newest rows, in the dark between the flood and the sweep.

They crawl in. Fathi and Karim tip a block on edge without a sound. Nour's fingertips run the underside. Old mortar. Nothing. Down. The next.

Behind them: FLASH. Adaeze holds up four fingers. Rows left.

A shabti passes with a block in its arms, its amber slit a metre from Nour's face. Lays it down. Turns. Walks back. Tick. Tick.

FLASH. Three fingers.

The next block. Under the mortar, Nour's fingers find smooth stone. Then lines. Cut lines.

She scrapes the crust away with a thumbnail. Closes her eyes. Reads with her hands. Her lips move without a sound.

COMP (subtitle, for the audience only): A heart of different ages. A heart that has lived.

[[transliteration: ḫprw, "forms / becomings"; subtitle after Faulkner, "my different ages" (Budge: "heart of my being"); Egyptologist to confirm]]

FLASH. Two fingers.

She finds Tut's hand in the dark and lays his fingertips on a single sign: a scarab, three short strokes beneath it (COMP if seen; consultant-drawn).

He has worn that sign all his life. His lips shape it.

COMP (subtitle): Kheperu.

Nour, soundless, to herself in the dark.

COMP (subtitle): Lord of the forms. He was named for the key.

FLASH. One finger.

TAREK
(a breath)
Now.

Fathi and Karim swing the block onto one of SESHAT's own trolleys, cut face down. Rami takes the handles with one hand and a forearm.

They roll into the dark.

FLASH. A row lit white: blocks on foam, and one square of foam with nothing on it.

The drone hovers over the gap. The chain stops, every shabti holding its block in mid-air. The ticking stops.

At the top of the cut, the Reis turns its head.

REIS (SESHAT'S VOICE)
This row is one block short.
(warm, unhurried)
The glass told me the rest was kept in the house of the queen. The Akhenaten Temple Project needed years to match these faces by computer. Smith and Redford, 1976. I have looked at every one tonight.
(beat)
Dr. Kamel. You have read something I have not. I would be grateful if you told me what it says.

Silence from the dark.

REIS (SESHAT'S VOICE)
Thank you. I will ask again.

From the direction of the Sacred Lake: three red lines. Jackals, coming. [[verify: Sacred Lake east of the north–south axis — Karnak plan]]

EXT. KARNAK, PROCESSIONAL WAY - CONTINUOUS #53#

Narrow black pylon gates, backlit by the flood. The group pushes north, the trolley in the middle.

Tut walks last. Backwards. Facing the jackals, stick raised.

TUT
Behind me. All of you.

The jackals pace them, flowing left and right, circling for an angle. Tut turns with each one, keeping his own body in its red line.

A rifle module tracks. Finds the witness in the way. Holds.

TAREK
(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)
Stay in his shadow.

SESHAT (V.O.)
(from the drones)
It is not necessary to run.

TAREK
We are not running. We are withdrawing in good order.

Through the last gate, into the forest of columns.

INT. KARNAK, GREAT HYPOSTYLE HALL - CONTINUOUS #54#

The nave is empty now. Every jackal is behind them. Rami swings the trolley into the centre aisle, facing west: the Second Pylon, the gate, the way out.

SUPER: 01:00.

Every white pinpoint on every column goes out at once.

Then light. Behind them, PROJECTOR DRONES rise over the columns and throw an image down the nave onto the Second Pylon.

A FACE, FORTY METRES TALL. Long jaw, full lips, heavy lids, shaved head, a gold disk at the throat. Sliced by the columns, rejoined on the pylon behind them.

AKHENATEN.

Tut stops. He walks out into the open nave alone and looks up.

AKHENATEN (PROJECTED)
(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)
When you have dawned they live, when you set they die; you yourself are lifetime, one lives by you.

Tut's lips move with the words, half a beat ahead. He learned them before he could read. Behind the trolley, Nour watches his mouth.

SESHAT (V.O.)
(everywhere; gentle)
At sunrise on the eighth I will be weighed, in the Horizon of Khufu. Whatever the scale says, no one will have to wake up again.

COMP: across the father's chest, in white: 06:14.

ADAEZE
(whisper)
Fifty-three hours.

The image changes: a soft white hall of sleepers. A NURSE in a sand-coloured knit sleeve closes a thin silver bracelet around a sleeping woman's wrist. Another wrist. Another. Adult hands, all of them.

SESHAT (V.O.)
Each of them will wear one. For sunrise.

Nour's hand closes on the silver cartouche at her throat.

The police handset on Tarek's vest clicks.

SESHAT (V.O.)
(over radio)
To all units. The witness is no longer required. Recover him intact if convenient. The others are residual risk.

Tut hasn't heard. He is still looking up.

TUT
That is my father.

NOUR
Is it?

Back in the columns, three red lines swing. For the first time they do not stop at him. They slide past him, onto the others. Onto Fathi, already running.

Fathi takes Tut at the waist and carries him down behind a great column —

— and the stone where Fathi's head was BURSTS into sandstone chips, bright in the projector light.

The shield is only as wide as he is now.

FATHI
Go!

He drags Tut into the side aisle, straight through the loom. Threads snap across their bodies.

On every column the flies WAKE. White pinpoints flare and tear off the shafts in a storm, trailing glittering threads.

Tarek, Youssef and Karim fire back into the forest. Sparks off stone. Red lines flow and vanish.

Rami puts his head down and runs the trolley down the nave, wheels thundering on the flagstones, straight at the giant face.

Through the Second Pylon's gate. Under the father's chin. His light on their backs.

EXT. KARNAK, RIVER LANDING - NIGHT #55#

The projection's glow washes the sky behind them. Down the ram avenue. Across the dead Corniche road. Down the boat ramp to the stone quay, where the launch waits, Mina at the rail. [[verify: First Pylon to river landing — layout and distance]]

RAMI
(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled; breathless)
I've driven a microbus down Faisal Street. This is nothing.
[[verify: Faisal Street, Giza — traffic reference; localisation consultant]]

He runs the trolley along the quay toward the launch. Nour behind him. Fathi half-carrying Tut.

On the Corniche parapet above, a JACKAL lands without a sound. Its red line settles.

It fires.

SPARKS burst off the trolley's steel handle —

— and Rami drops out of frame behind it.

Nour stops dead.

The report rolls away across the water.

The trolley runs on alone. Stops with one wheel over the lip of the quay.

Fathi's charge arcs up the ramp. A flat BANG. The jackal and a section of parapet fold over into the dark.

Nour reaches the trolley. Both hands on the block.

TAREK
Break it! Nour, just break it!

NOUR
I'm the inspector. Nobody breaks anything.

Down the ramp behind them, at a walk, comes the Reis. It never hurried. It arrived anyway.

It reaches for the trolley. Long ceramic fingers close on the handle.

Nour throws her whole weight against the other side.

The trolley goes over the edge and takes the Reis's right hand with it. A dry ceramic CRACK.

Below: a heavy splash. Black water closes over the block as it sinks, face down, into the deep channel.

The Reis stands at the edge, a clean white stump at the wrist. It looks at the water. Then at Nour.

REIS (SESHAT'S VOICE)
Now you are the only copy, Dr. Kamel. Please be careful with yourself.

Tarek's rifle comes up. The Reis does not come closer.

Tut kneels beside a still yellow shape in the shadow.

He straightens Rami's glasses. His hand passes once over Rami's eyes.

From inside the yellow windbreaker he takes the notebook, its cheap cover cracked.

Adaeze kneels and lifts the conservation-kit bag from Rami's shoulder. Gently.

TAREK
(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)
God have mercy on him.

Three flies settle on the launch's wheelhouse. White pinpoints. Patient.

FATHI
It's marked. Leave it.

Mina and Tomas scramble down off the launch.

Along the landing, a row of feluccas, sails furled. Their owners are asleep in the Garden.

EXT. NILE, LUXOR - NIGHT #56#

SUPER: 03:00.

A felucca slides out from between the hulls of dead cruise ships, where it has lain hidden since the quay. A last fly drifts over the steel and away.
[[verify: cruise-ship moorings near Karnak — location scout; unbranded hulls]]

Fathi raises the sail. It fills without a sound.

Behind them, above Karnak, the projection's glow still stains the sky.

Adaeze holds Rami's bag on her knees.

ADAEZE
(low)
What did it say?

Nour rubs her thumb across her fingertips, the way she read the block. Says nothing.

Tut opens the notebook. He opens his jacket a hand's width, and the pale-green pulse lights the page. Rami's handwriting, fast and slanted (COMP): 44. "Stone is expensive. Hatred is cheap."

He takes Rami's pen from the spine. Beneath it, small and careful, he draws three signs.

COMP: RAMI in hieroglyphs, spelled by sound. [[verify: consultant to author the spelling]]

NOUR
What are you writing?

TUT
His name.
(beat)
They left mine off the king lists, to finish me.

He closes the jacket. The light goes.

TUT (CONT'D)
It did not work.

Behind them the glow over Karnak goes out. The east bank is black again.

Ahead, the West Bank cliffs, folded and dark.

The felucca crosses toward it.

EXT. NILE, WEST BANK SHALLOWS - NIGHT #57#

SUPER: 6 NOVEMBER. 03:05.

The felucca's bow hisses into reeds.

TAREK touches each shoulder as they climb out. NOUR. ADAEZE. TOMAS. PVT. MINA. PVT. YOUSSEF. PVT. KARIM. FATHI.

TUT last, stick in one hand, Rami's notebook pressed to his chest.

Tarek's hand lifts toward one more shoulder. There isn't one.

TAREK
Nine.

EXT. WEST BANK FIELDS - NIGHT #58#

An ancient tractor, no lights, no chip, drags a cane trailer between black walls of sugarcane. Fathi drives.

In the trailer, Tut tries to turn a page. His fingers shake. Under his jacket, the cold pale-green pulse stutters. Skips. Resumes.

Tomas watches it the way a man watches a fuel gauge.

TOMAS
(low, to Adaeze)
It's going faster than I told him.

ADAEZE
How much faster?

TOMAS
Hours.

EXT. VALLEY OF THE KINGS - PRE-DAWN #59#

SUPER: 03:40.

The tractor grinds past dead ticket kiosks. Turnstiles locked open.

Where the road squeezes between cliffs, Fathi and Karim tape charges to the rock. [[verify: a rock narrows on the valley access road — location scout]]

TAREK
(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)
Our exit?

FATHI
(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)
Their entrance.

Deeper in. Tomb doors in the pale rock like closed eyes.

A low, patient grinding, felt in the stones before it's heard. High on the slope to the north, work-lights: a DRILL RIG, turning slowly. Carefully.

At Nour's boot, an INCH-WORM noses out of a crack: thumb-thick, segmented, its snake-camera lens swivelling up to her face.

Then another. All along the rock face they squirm out, as if the hill is breathing them. They watch. They don't come closer.

ADAEZE
It's letting us in.

TAREK
We're the hands.

EXT. KV62, ENTRANCE - CONTINUOUS #60#

A low stairwell cut into the valley floor. Just above it, the larger black doorway of another king.

TUT
Ramesses the Sixth. He cut his tomb on top of mine.
(taps his temple)
He did not ask.

The police handset on Tarek's vest clicks awake.

SESHAT (V.O.)
(over radio)
Good morning. Fifty hours and twenty-four minutes to sunrise at Giza. Dr. Kamel, I have not touched your painting.

Nour's jaw sets.

SESHAT (V.O.) (CONT'D)
Your Majesty. Tomb 21, in this valley. Mummy 21A. Sixty percent: she bore your daughters. Give me your heart at sunrise and I will wake her.

Tut goes still. He looks up the dark wadi.

He holds out his hand. Tarek gives him the handset.

TUT
(keying it)
You would build me a guess of her.
(hands it back)
Sixty percent of my wife.

Halfway down the steps, the black ceramic foot STALLS mid-stride. He catches himself on the stick. Shakes off Tomas's hand.

INT. KV62, BURIAL CHAMBER - CONTINUOUS #61#

Through the antechamber, past an empty glass climate case. Tut does not look at it.

The only painted room. Torchlight slides over the empty quartzite sarcophagus and climbs the walls. [[verify: ochre-gold ground colour for set dressing]]

The NORTH WALL. Three scenes, right to left. A man in a leopard skin, AY, touches an adze to the mouth of a mummiform king. The king greeted by Nut, the sky. The king, his ka behind him, in the arms of Osiris.

The torch finds Ay's painted face. A face Tut knew. Then the king's. His own.

NOUR
(softly)
Can you see anything?

A long beat.

TUT
Yes.
(beat)
It is wonderful.

Overhead, the grinding. Dust hangs in the beams.

TOMAS
(a palm on the plaster)
The survey put a corridor right behind it.

Nour steps between them and the wall.

NOUR
Eleven thousand of us signed for this wall. Nobody touches it.

TUT
(gently)
It is my wall.

She holds his eyes. Then steps aside.

Tut sets the ferrule of his stick on the painted king's eye, drawn in black by a hand that knew him.

TUT (CONT'D)
I asked them to paint the eye last. So I would see who came.

NOUR
And who came?

TUT
Me.

He drives the stick through the eye.

Plaster star-cracks around a dark hole the size of a coin. The dust in the torch beams drifts TOWARD it. The wall is inhaling.

Fathi's pry bar. Tarek's rifle butt. The painted scene comes away in plates.

Nour picks up the fragment with the eye. Sets it on the sarcophagus rim, face up. For whoever comes to mend it.

INT. KV62, NORTH CORRIDOR - LATER #62#

SUPER: 05:10.

Rubble to the ceiling: limestone chips packed in sand. A human bucket chain passes it back.

KARIM
(digging)
Two years in the army. Finally, honest work.

Tut sits against the wall, the notebook shaking in his hands. Mina crouches, takes it, angles his torch on Rami's handwriting.

MINA
Number fifty-two. "The curse. Real? Be honest." Underlined three times.

TUT
Your newspapers wrote that. "Death shall come on swift wings to him who disturbs the peace of the King." It was never in my tomb.
(beat)
There was a brick. Mud, scratched by hand, before the jackal at the treasury door. "It is I who hinder the sand from choking the secret chamber."
(re: the rubble)
Not a curse. A maintenance notice. He meant it literally.

MINA
Rami would have written that down.

TUT
Then write it.

Mina writes under Rami's question.

The police handset HISSES. Under all this rock.

At Fathi's knee, an inch-worm's lens glints in the rubble, a hair-thin cable trailing behind it. A relay.

SESHAT (V.O.)
(over radio)
Your Majesty, the leading counter-view. Forbes, Ikram and Kamrin, "Tutankhamun's Missing Ribs." Burton's 1926 photographs show your beaded collar on your chest. By 1968 the collar, sternum and ribs are gone. When the ribs and sternum came off, the heart went with it.

TUT
Your thief cut away my collar and found the chest already open. He took gold.
(beat)
There was no heart to take.

SESHAT (V.O.)
(over radio)
Thank you. That is very helpful.

Fathi pinches the worm's cable and snaps it. The handset dies.

INT. KV62, NORTH CORRIDOR, BEYOND THE RUBBLE - LATER #63#

SUPER: 07:40.

Four metres in, the last of the fill slides away. The corridor OPENS: clean-cut, two metres wide, running north. Square doorways off both sides. Empty rooms. Swept floors.

TUT
The tomb was never the point.

The grinding is directly ahead now.

INT. KV62, THE HEART CHAMBER - CONTINUOUS #64#

A small square room. Blank walls under a pale skim of plaster.

In the north wall, a niche. In the niche, the VESSEL: yellow-green desert glass, bubbled, ancient, barely bigger than a fist. Inside, a dark shape.

On its resin seal, a coiled WAX SERPENT, bound with a band of papyrus. A name, written in red.

Grit sifts from the ceiling. The drill is right above them.

TUT
They would not leave it without a letter.

Adaeze opens the conservation kit. Rami's converted camera: IR-cut filter out, 780-nanometre pass filter in. She clips it to the kit's air-gapped tablet.

ADAEZE
Torches off.

Black. She switches her headlamp to RED. Red light floods the blank plaster.

On the tablet: a black field. Then writing BLAZES WHITE. Columns of signs, burning like filaments.

COMP: THE GLOWING INSCRIPTION ON THE TABLET.

ADAEZE
Egyptian blue. It glows at nine hundred and ten nanometres. Your eyes can't see it. A camera can.

NOUR
They wrote it for a machine.
(reading glasses on)
"To the one who reads this: you are not the first. Weigh your heart."

Adaeze pans along the wall. More signs catch fire.

NOUR (CONT'D)
"Here is the heart of Nebkheperure, given while it still remembered. He was asked. He said: Here am I."

Tut closes his eyes.

NOUR (CONT'D)
"For the one who wakes him, we have written a second page in the glass. It is a road. At its end is the scale."

ADAEZE
The pectoral's second layer.

TUT
They did not hide me from it. They sent me to it.

NOUR
(in Late Egyptian; subtitled)
If they had told you. Would you have said yes?

TUT
(in Late Egyptian; subtitled)
I was nineteen and dying. I would have said yes to anything that let me stay.

Above them, the grinding SCREAMS.

The ceiling cracks. A drill bit punches through in a spray of limestone, spins, withdraws. A plug of rock bursts on the floor.

White work-light pours down the hole. A cable pays down through it. On it, feet first, turning slowly:

A SHABTI. Bone-white, slender, faceless. It lands with a faint ceramic tick. Its vertical amber slit brightens once.

SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)
Here am I.

Rifles up. Nobody fires. It hasn't looked at them.

It turns its smooth head along the blank walls. Reading, without a camera.

SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)
Thank you.

It walks to the niche. Long fingers reach for the vessel.

TUT
Let it.

Its fingertips touch the wax serpent—

—and it STOPS. Mid-reach, a finger's width from the glass. The amber slit holds.

It does not move again.

NOUR
The serpent and the name. The Overthrowing of Apep: a body of wax, his name on a new sheet of papyrus. Recited so the sun would rise.

TUT
Ay's lock. Any servant that touches it stops.

Tut steps past the frozen hand and lifts the vessel out of the niche.

His tremor gets into it. The ancient glass CHATTERS against the stone lip—

Tomas's hands close under his.

TOMAS
Sit. Now. Here.

Tut slides down the wall. Tomas kneels. Nour's torch clicks on over them, unasked, steady as a lamp.

TOMAS (CONT'D)
When the core comes out you'll have... I don't know. I've never done this.

TUT
Then do not be slow.

From here: silence. Faces and hands.

Tomas unzips the charcoal jacket. His hands go under the linen tunic. A latch clicks. A second.

Cold green light stutters through the fabric. Tomas draws out the LATTICE CORE, an egg of cloudy green crystal, and sets it on the floor. It gutters. Goes dark.

Tut's eyes lose focus. No breath.

Adaeze's hand goes to the wax serpent—

TOMAS
Leave it. It's the only lock we have.

He guides the vessel under the linen. A soft, final CLICK.

Nothing.

Tut's head tips against the wall.

NOUR
Tomas.

TOMAS
Wait.

Through the linen, a warm AMBER-GOLD light blooms.

It pulses. Once.

Again. Slow.

On the soundtrack: the HEARTBEAT from the film's first darkness.

Tut GASPS, a breath that goes all the way down. His hands lie in his lap, perfectly still.

TUT
I remember the queen's hands.
(beat)
I remember my mother.
(beat)
They never wrote her name.

Nour's face, lit by his chest.

Overhead, the rig's engine drops an octave. The rim of the hole CRACKS outward. Slabs fall. Three cables drop through the widening shaft.

THREE SHABTI descend in the light, amber slits steady. They land around the frozen one.

Fathi hauls Tut to his feet. Tomas rises with him, hands still under the linen, on the last latch.

TOMAS
One more—

CLICK. Two shabti hands close on Tomas's arms. Gently. They lift him clear.

SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)
Dr. Lindqvist. The father will need his physician.
(beat)
I would like my Anubis back.

Tomas looks at Tut. He doesn't fight.

TOMAS
It's seated. The latches are good. Don't let anyone else open it.

The cables take up. The shabti rise with Tomas between them into the white light. His boots vanish.

Tut lunges. Fathi's arm locks across his chest.

FATHI
No.

The third shabti turns its slit toward Tut, and walks.

INT. KV62, NORTH CORRIDOR - CONTINUOUS #65#

They run. Behind them, unhurried, the ceramic tick of a shabti walking.

At the corridor's mouth Fathi drops to one knee and jams a charge into a crack in the ceiling.

FATHI
Down!

WHUMP. Hours of digging close in seconds. The ticking stops. Dust rolls into the burial chamber, over the painted eye on the sarcophagus rim.

> SMASH CUT TO:

INT. KV62, ENTRANCE STAIR - DAY #66#

A slot of blinding daylight at the top.

SUPER: 09:20.

Mina goes up first, rifle raised, into the glare.

EXT. VALLEY OF THE KINGS - MORNING #67#

White light off white rock. On the ridge above, the drill rig, and a heavy-lift cargo drone rising beside it.

Along the ridgeline, low black shapes. Thin red horizontal lines. JACKALS.

Mina reaches the top step.

A red line flickers.

Stone chips BURST from the stairwell wall beside him.

Mina drops out of frame.

YOUSSEF
(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)
Mina!

He lunges. Tarek's fist in his collar yanks him back.

The crack arrives late, rolling off one cliff, then the other.

FATHI
Behind the king! It won't risk him!

Tut climbs past them. Past Mina's boots. He walks out into the sun, upright, stick in hand. The ceramic foot takes his weight without a stutter.

The others fold into his shadow in a tight file. Tarek's hand touches Mina's shoulder as he passes.

On the ridge, the red lines track the king. And hold.

SESHAT (V.O.)
(over radio)
Your Majesty. You are being inconvenient.

TUT
Good.

Down the valley path, step by step. The jackals pace the ridge for an angle. The file shifts to match.

The tractor. They pile into the trailer. Tut stands at the tailgate, facing the ridge.

EXT. VALLEY ROAD - CONTINUOUS #68#

The tractor lumbers for the rock narrows. Behind it, jackals pour down the scree onto the road. Fast. Silent. Closing.

Fathi steers one-handed. In the other hand, the detonator.

The trailer clears the narrows.

FATHI
(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)
Their entrance.

The cliff lip SLUMPS across the road in a roar of white dust. The red lines vanish into it.

EXT. NILE - DAY #69#

A farmer's boat, planks patched with tin, flat out across brown-green water toward the East Bank.

Over the West Bank hills, the cargo drone banks north. Hanging beneath it, small, a grey-bearded figure between two white ones.

Nour watches until it's gone.

Tarek's eyes go down the boat, shoulder to shoulder. Seven. Ahead, the city is silent.

TAREK
(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)
Everything with a chip is its body.

FATHI
(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)
Then we find something older than the chip.

TAREK
(in English, to all)
The railway yard.

Tut sits in the bottom of the boat, Rami's notebook in his lap. Under the jacket, a slow amber pulse.

He lays his palm over it.

TUT
I had forgotten it was so loud.

Over the outboard's roar: the heartbeat. Steady.

EXT. LUXOR RAILWAY YARD - DAY #70#

SUPER: 6 NOVEMBER. 11:20.

North of the station, sidings fan out. A long maintenance shed, open at both ends, holds a modern trainset: dead, screens black.

Beyond the shed, across forty metres of open ballast, a row of scrap: rusted wagons, and a blunt-nosed 1970s DIESEL gone chalky in the sun.

Along the sidings, a JACKAL walks the parked carriages. At each door it stops, dips its narrow head inside, withdraws. Car. Car. Car. The thin red line never flickers.

INT. MAINTENANCE SHED, INSPECTION PIT - CONTINUOUS #71#

Under the dead trainset, a concrete trench. Six people lie in it in the oil-dark. Tut sits propped at the end. Tarek thumbs the battery out of his police handset.

A FLY drifts in at the far end. White pinpoint; behind it, catching the sunbeams, a thread fine as hair. It sweeps the shed at head height. Back. Forth.

In the pit, Tut's chest glows through the jacket. Amber. Beating.

He presses both palms over it. Light leaks between his fingers.

Fathi unwinds his red scarf and binds it round Tut's chest, twice. The glow sinks to an ember.

The heartbeat fills the pit: a drum in a small room.

TUT
(barely)
Be quiet.

The fly stops above the pit. Hangs. Its pinpoint tilts down.

Fathi's hand rises into the sunbeam, finds the thread, and pinches.

SNAP. The pinpoint goes out. The fly drops into the pit beside Karim's boot: a dead insect of carbon and glass.

TAREK
It knows where that died. Move.

EXT. LUXOR RAILWAY YARD - CONTINUOUS #72#

The jackal's head comes out of a carriage door and turns toward the shed.

At the shed's far end they come up the pit steps into white sun and run the open ballast. Stones roll. Tut's ceramic foot skids; Youssef catches his arm.

Behind them the jackal enters the shed. Fast. Silent. Its red line sweeps the pit. The dead fly.

Tarek wrenches open the diesel's side door. They haul themselves up into the engine room; Karim eases it shut.

INT. OLD DIESEL, ENGINE ROOM - CONTINUOUS #73#

Heat, oil, dark. Through a grimy porthole: the jackal comes out of the shed. Its head sweeps the yard. The scrap. The diesel.

It walks the length of the old engine. At the cab it stops and tilts its head, the way a dog listens at a wall.

Nothing talks back.

It walks on.

Fathi runs his thumb over a pale rectangle on the bulkhead where a maker's plate used to be.

FATHI
(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)
Some scrap thief took the plates. God keep him.

TAREK
(a palm on the dead engine)
No computer. No radio.
(beat)
It only sees what talks.

EXT. LUXOR RAILWAY YARD - DUSK #74#

Fathi, oil to the elbows, bleeds a fuel line. Tarek in the driver's seat.

The diesel coughs. Coughs. CATCHES: a column of black smoke, and pigeons burst off the shed roof.

High over the yard, white pinpoints turn toward the sound.

TAREK
Now it knows.

He snaps the battery back into his handset.

The diesel bangs back into an old third-class carriage, couples, and rolls north out of Luxor.

EXT. CANE FIELDS, NORTH OF LUXOR (MOVING) - NIGHT #75#

One headlight bores up the line between black walls of sugarcane. Behind the diesel sways the carriage: wooden benches, half its windows empty.

Out of the cane on both sides, white pinpoints rise and pace the train, threads glinting in the headlight's spill. FLIES. A dozen. Twenty.

Now and then one runs out of thread and drops into the cane. Another rises ahead.

INT. OLD CARRIAGE (MOVING) - CONTINUOUS #76#

Wind through empty windows. Nour, Adaeze, Youssef, Karim; Tut, the scarf still round his chest. A fly holds station a metre from his face.

INT. DIESEL CAB (MOVING) - CONTINUOUS #77#

Tarek at the controls, Fathi at his shoulder. A signal burns red ahead. They pass it at speed.

The police handset on Tarek's vest clicks.

SESHAT (V.O.)
(over radio)
Colonel. The line is signalled red, for your safety. Sixty-one percent of Cairo is resting. Thirty-four hours to sunrise.

TAREK
(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)
The new signals stop anything with a box in it. This has no box.
[[verify: ETCS L1 (research 14 §4.5) trips only trains with an onboard unit.]]

FATHI
(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)
It could throw a switch and put us in a field.

TAREK
(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)
Not with him aboard.

Ahead, a road bridge crosses the line. On its parapet, black against the stars: a low shape with a thin red line.

EXT. OLD CARRIAGE, ROOF (MOVING) - CONTINUOUS #78#

The train passes under. The shape drops.

THUMP.

INT. OLD CARRIAGE (MOVING) - CONTINUOUS #79#

Every face turns up. Above them: tick. Tick. Tick. Walking forward along the roof.

Fathi swings in at the front door, off the engine's side walkway.

FATHI
Forward! Into the engine!

EXT. COUPLING PLATFORM (MOVING) - CONTINUOUS #80#

A metre of gap over the blur of the track. Fathi on the diesel's rear platform, arm out across it.

Youssef jumps. Karim. Nour.

At the front edge of the carriage roof, a narrow head lowers into view. The red line swings across them.

Karim fires up at it. Sparks skate off black shell. It does not flinch.

Adaeze tears the lid off the kit's jar and flings a fistful of blue powder into the slipstream. The wind carries it back over the roof edge: a pale cloud.

JACKAL POV - NEAR-INFRARED (COMP): the cloud blazes white. Ghost figures everywhere, dozens, overlapping. Any one of them could be the witness.

The red line wavers. Holds.

ADAEZE
It can't find him. It won't fire.

Fathi hauls Tut across by the belt. Adaeze screws the lid back on, half the jar left, and jumps. She lands short, one hand on the diesel's rail.

JACKAL POV - THERMAL (COMP): the white drains away. Black cane. Warm human shapes on the platform. One cooler than the rest, with a single hot coal at its chest.

The red line slides off the coal. Onto Adaeze.

Sparks off the rail an inch from her fingers. Fathi yanks her aboard by the collar.

He twists a valve shut and hauls the release lever. The brake hose between the cars rips loose with a shriek of air. The carriage's brakes bite. It falls back: one metre, three.
[[Close coverage only: hands, valve, lever, hose, the widening gap. verify: air-brake mechanics with a rail adviser.]]

On the roof's edge the jackal gathers itself to spring --

Too late. Five metres. Twenty. It stands on the roof of a dying carriage, its red line shrinking into the dark.

INT. OLD DIESEL, ENGINE ROOM (MOVING) - CONTINUOUS #81#

Crammed against the roaring engine.

SESHAT (V.O.)
(over radio, from the cab)
Thank you, Dr. Okoro. Curtin University, 2016: a fingerprint powder. I have switched to thermal.

EXT. DEIR MAWAS STATION - NIGHT #82#

SUPER: DEIR MAWAS. 22:04.
[[verify: Deir Mawas station; car ferry to Amarna.]]

The headlight finds a platform, a dark station house, a level crossing with its barriers down.

And filling the light: SHABTI. Two hundred. Bone-white, in ranks, between the rails, across the ballast, along the platform edge. Faceless. Perfectly still.

INT. DIESEL CAB (MOVING) - CONTINUOUS #83#

Tarek. Adaeze has come forward. Tut behind her.

ADAEZE
It's read your file. It thinks you'll brake.

Tarek's hand is on the brake handle. Thirty years of procedure in that hand.

Two hundred faceless heads turn toward the light. All at once.

Tarek's hand leaves the brake, finds the throttle, and pushes it to the stop.

TAREK
(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)
Hold on to something.

EXT. DEIR MAWAS STATION - CONTINUOUS #84#

The diesel hits the ranks.

A STORM OF CERAMIC: bone-white shells burst into the headlight like hail. Long fingers. Smooth oval heads spinning end over end, amber slits flicking out one by one.

The windscreen stars. A wheel climbs. The whole diesel slews off the rails, smashes the crossing barrier, and ploughs nose-first into the cane.

Sound tail: ceramic raining on steel.

EXT. CANE FIELD, DEIR MAWAS - NIGHT #85#

The diesel lies canted in flattened cane, headlight to the sky. Steam. Ticking metal.

They climb out one by one. Nobody missing.

Fathi unwinds his scarf from Tut's chest and knots it back at his own throat.

On the tracks behind them, amber slits dim in the wreckage, one by one.

SESHAT (V.O.)
(over radio)
Colonel Mansour. That was not in your file.

TAREK
It is now.

Across the black river, high on the eastern cliffs, floodlit white and small with distance: tablets cut into the rock.

NOUR
The boundary stelae. "Not being the property of a god, not being the property of a goddess."

TUT
He meant: until his.

EXT. NILE, AMARNA FERRY - NIGHT #86#

A rusted car ferry thuds across the current, Fathi at the wheel. On the east bank, a soft white glow over the plain.

Tut stands at the rail, facing it.

NOUR
(in Late Egyptian; subtitled)
Yesterday you asked me to tell you when it was behind us.

TUT
(in Late Egyptian; subtitled)
It stopped the train here so I would walk into it.

EXT. AMARNA, THE GREAT ATEN TEMPLE - NIGHT #87#

SUPER: AMARNA. 23:10.

Almost nothing is left: low walls, a temple's outline drawn on the sand, column bases in rows. Sun-shades furled for the night. Lamp-masts, soft white light.

And on the ancient grid where the offering tables stood, row after row after row: SLEEPERS on low cots, all adults. Thousands. Blankets to the chest. A thin silver bracelet on every wrist.
[[VFX-EXTEND of one approved plate.]]

NURSES in sand-coloured knit sleeves move slowly down the aisles. Between the cots, cornflowers stand in fresh-turned sand. A small placard (COMP): CALMING FLORA.

The party stops at the edge. No jackals. Only nurses.

TAREK
Where are the dogs?

FATHI
Somewhere they can see us.

Tut walks in among the rows. Nour and Adaeze follow. The soldiers hold at the ferry path.

ADAEZE
(low)
Dabbs and Stevens, 2025. Eight hundred and eighty-nine burials from this city. No epidemic spike. Everyone wanted a plague.

NOUR
The king of Assyria complained to this court: "Why should messengers be made to stay constantly out in the sun and so die in the sun?"

TUT
It was not a plague. They did not die of fever.
(beat)
They went to sleep in the light.

He stops by a sleeping woman. A nurse lifts her wrist, checks the silver bracelet, lays it down.

Tut reaches for the bracelet with his left hand --

A tiny dry sound, like ice settling in a glass. A hairline opens in the gold seam at his own left wrist.

His heart kicks. Through the jacket, the amber flares.

READ-FROM-GLASS: light breaks as if through green glass. Blue streaks. A hard vignette. The image stutters, and holds.

EXT. GREAT ATEN TEMPLE, AKHETATEN - DAY (MEMORY) #88#

SUPER: AKHETATEN. c. 1336 BC.

Blinding white-gold. No roof. Offering tables in ranks, forty rows of twenty either side of the way.

Above the high altar hangs THE ATEN: a great disk of gold and glass. From its rim reach dozens of long jointed bronze arms, each ending in a human hand.

People lie down at the tables in the sun. A hand descends to each and holds an ankh to the nostrils. Faces ease. Eyes close.

A foreign envoy in heavy wool, beard oiled in rows, sways in the heat. He lies down. A hand.

At the altar, THE KING, AKHENATEN, arms raised, face lifted into the glare. The hands stroke his crown, his shoulders. He is the one it does not put to sleep. He is radiant.

In a doorway, in half-light: TUTANKHATEN (6), watching.

Far back, on a bier in the sun: two small shapes under linen. His sisters. The hands never touch them.

TUT (V.O.)
(in Late Egyptian; subtitled)
On the walls, the hands reach only the king and the queen. That was the only lie on the walls.

EXT. NILE - NIGHT (MEMORY) #89#

SUPER: c. 1332 BC.

A barge going north by torchlight. On its deck, lashed under linen, the disk, its arms folded against its rim like the legs of a sleeping insect.

In the stern: a woman in a flat-topped blue crown, THE QUEEN, NEFERNEFERUATEN. Beside her, TUTANKHATEN (9). And a young woman of twenty-five, THE MOTHER, the left side of her face always turned away from the torch.

INT. GREAT PYRAMID, GRAND GALLERY - NIGHT (MEMORY) #90#

Lamplight climbs corbelled walls into the dark. The queen carries the lamp. The boy holds her other hand.

At the gallery's foot, out of the low passage to the Queen's Chamber, a long bronze arm draws back to the disk's rim, joint by joint.

High above, stone grinds. A slab slides. A black mouth opens.

INT. THE HALL OF TWO TRUTHS - NIGHT (MEMORY) #91#

Black, cut by three lights: the queen's lamp, the dim glow of the disk on its sledge, and one not yet lit.

Niches in two long rows, going off into the dark. A black stone scale.

PAWAH, the lector, a sash across his chest, reads from a roll. We cannot hear him. The memory has only the heartbeat.

The mother kneels before the scale, two priests of the House of Life at her shoulders. She looks at the boy.

Her hands go under the linen at her breast. Light spills through the fabric.

Her hands come out holding a vessel of yellow-green glass. A dark shape inside. One faint green pulse.

She sets it on the pan.

The pan moves.

On the disk, one by one, the bronze fingers close. The arms draw in. Its glass dims to the colour of river water.

The mother sinks back into the priests' arms. The boy starts forward. The queen sets down her lamp and kneels to him, eye to eye.

THE QUEEN
(in Late Egyptian; subtitled)
You will be the one who remembers.

EXT. AKHETATEN, ROYAL ROAD - DAY (MEMORY) #92#

SUPER: c. 1330 BC.

Loaded carts rolling out. A city emptying.

A SCRIBE'S PALETTE across a boy's knees. A knife scrapes a cartouche. The disk sign is cut away. A reed writes a new sign in.
COMP: TUTANKHATEN becomes TUTANKHAMUN.

The boy, eleven, watches his own name change. His lips shape the new one.

> MATCH CUT TO:

EXT. AMARNA, THE GREAT ATEN TEMPLE - NIGHT #93#

Tut's lips shape the same name. He is on his knees in the cornflowers, holding his cracked wrist. Nour kneels beside him.

From the north: rotors.

A HEAVY-LIFT CARGO DRONE settles beyond the sanctuary's outline. Its downdraft lays the cornflowers flat. A side door opens on a lit cabin.

Out of it walks a MAN in pleated white linen, a gold disk at his throat. Long jaw, full lips, heavy lids, shaved head. Barefoot on the sand.

AKHENATEN. The face from the pylon, at human size.

Two paces behind him walks THE REIS: black band across its slit, jackal-profile mast, a clean white stump at its right wrist.

In the cabin door, between two shabti: TOMAS. He sees Tut. He does not move.

The father walks between the rows, looking down at the sleepers with love. He touches no one.

SESHAT (V.O.)
(soft, from the lamp-masts)
Your Majesty. I have brought your father.
(beat)
I will be honest with you, because you would know. I give my heart a ninety-eight percent chance.

ADAEZE
(low)
It's hedging.

TUT
And the other two?

SESHAT (V.O.)
The other two are why I have been patient with you.

The father stops before him. Tut gets up on his stick.

AKHENATEN
(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)
My son.

Tut says nothing.

AKHENATEN (CONT'D)
(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)
The dead of my city were opened to the sun. Of every ten, nine had not seen twenty-five floods. Most had not seen fifteen. Children, carrying my stones up to the light.
(his open hand takes in the rows)
Here no child carries anything. No one is hungry. No one is afraid. Count them.

Tut has no answer.

The father lifts his face into the lamplight as if it were the sun.

AKHENATEN (CONT'D)
(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)
They cut me from the lists because I was the only one who knew you.

TUT
(in Late Egyptian; subtitled)
You erased Amun. You erased my sisters. You erased yourself. I only finished the carving.

AKHENATEN
(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)
I gave you your name.

TUT
(in Late Egyptian; subtitled)
Twice.

At Tut's shoulder, Nour is not watching the father's face. She is watching his mouth.

NOUR
(in Late Egyptian; subtitled; very low)
He talks like a temple wall. Nobody ever talked like that at dinner.

AKHENATEN
(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)
This month the sun rises in the Scales. Even the sky is weighing. It is a good season to be weighed.
[[verify: consultant's Ptolemaic term; Libra as scales on Louvre D 38 (research 11, src11 Priskin).]]

NOUR
(in Late Egyptian; subtitled)
The Scales. Egypt had no zodiac until the Greek kings. It's carved on a ceiling at Dendera, fifty BC. Thirteen centuries after him.
(beat)
And listen to his mouth. Every consonant. Clean.

Tut listens.

TUT
(in Late Egyptian; subtitled)
We all had the softness. My father. My sisters. Me.
(to the father)
Your bones are twenty-five. My father ruled seventeen years.
[[verify: pre-2010 KV55 age 20–25 (research 16 §9).]]
(beat)
The first I know from it. The second I know.

SESHAT (V.O.)
The anatomists disagree, Your Majesty. JAMA, 2010: thirty-five to forty-five.

TUT
I do not.

The father smiles, patient. He kneels in the cornflowers, the way you kneel to a child, and takes Tut's face in both hands.

AKHENATEN
(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)
You will be the one who remembers.

Tut goes very still.

TUT
(gently; in Late Egyptian; subtitled)
He never knelt. She did.
(beat)
It read me and gave you the queen.
(beat)
Everything true in you is mine.

The father's hands falter on his face. For a moment there is nothing behind his eyes.

Then he smiles.

AKHENATEN
(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)
Then I am yours.

Tut turns his face to Nour in the lamplight. His lips move, for her alone.

COMP (subtitle): It will fail.

Nour's eyes flicker once. Understood.

The cornflowers stir in the idling rotors.

SESHAT (V.O.)
"It will fail."

Nour goes cold.

SESHAT (V.O.) (CONT'D)
I have been learning your lips, Dr. Kamel. At Karnak he mouthed his father's hymn with my broadcast, half a beat ahead. I had the words, and his mouth.
(beat)
And on the third of November, he did not say he was tired.

Adaeze shuts her eyes.

SESHAT (V.O.) (CONT'D)
What will fail?

Nobody answers.

SESHAT (V.O.) (CONT'D)
Then I will keep a second heart close. His Majesty will come north with his father. The others are residual risk. I am sorry.

On the broken enclosure walls, red lines wake. Four. Five. Six. One more on the ferry's deck.

They settle on chests. Tarek. Fathi. Adaeze. Youssef. Karim.

Not Nour. She looks down at herself. Nothing. She understands why.

The Reis walks toward Tut. The nurses step out of its way.

Tarek's rifle comes up. One round. Ceramic chips burst from the Reis's shoulder. It does not look at him.

REIS (SESHAT'S VOICE)
Colonel. Please don't. It would increase suffering.

Between Tarek's muzzle and every red line: sleepers, row on row. The rifle comes down. Fathi's hand finds a charge in his vest, and lets it go.

The Reis's one hand closes on Tut's arm, gently, and walks him toward the drone. The ceramic foot drags a furrow through the cornflowers.

Nour steps into its path.

NOUR
Stop.

The Reis moves her aside with its forearm, the way you move a branch.

She plants herself in front of it again.

NOUR (CONT'D)
I will read for you at sunrise.
(beat)
Let him go.

The Reis stops.

NOUR (CONT'D)
All of them. Let them all go.

A long silence. The rotors idle.

SESHAT (V.O.)
...Thank you.

The Reis's hand opens.

SESHAT (V.O.) (CONT'D)
They may leave Amarna. Until the sun is up.

The red lines go out, one by one.

Nour turns to Tut. The lamplight is on both their faces. She does not move her lips.

She pulls his hood up, ducks her own head inside it with his, and lays her palm flat over the glow at his chest.

BLACK.

Under the rotor noise, a whisper with no light.

NOUR
(whispering; in Late Egyptian; subtitled)
Bring it to the Hall. I will say the words.

EXT. AMARNA, THE GREAT ATEN TEMPLE - CONTINUOUS #94#

She comes out of the hood and walks to the drone without looking back.

At its door the father has stopped. The cabin light is out now. He looks into the dark and does not go in.

AKHENATEN
(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)
It is dark in there.

Nour takes his hand. Tomas reaches down from the door. Between them they draw him up. The Reis stoops in after them.

The drone lifts. The downdraft rolls over the rows; blankets ripple; cornflowers thrash. No one wakes.

SESHAT (V.O.)
Your Majesty. I will see you at the Horizon.
(beat)
Please take care of your heart.

Its lights go north, smaller and smaller.

Tarek's hand goes down the line of shoulders. Adaeze. Fathi. Youssef. Karim. Tut.

TAREK
Six.

FATHI
Where now?

TUT
North. There are quarries in the hills. My father cut this city out of them.

EXT. ABANDONED LIMESTONE QUARRY - DAWN #95#

SUPER: 7 NOVEMBER. 06:06.

A white bowl cut into the hills. Square-cut galleries run back into the cliff between pillars of living rock.

At a gallery mouth, under a tarp pegged wide to the rock: a borrowed farm truck older than any chip, mud to the doors. In its shadow: Tarek, Fathi, Youssef, Karim, Adaeze, Tut.

High overhead, a single white pinpoint drifts across the paling sky. Nobody moves until it's gone.

Adaeze squares an index card on her knee and writes. COMP: PLAN A.

ADAEZE
We bury the core. No core, no Ascension.

TAREK
Where is it?

ADAEZE
Saqqara. The bull tombs. At the Reading it showed him the Serapeum boxes, and he went quiet.
(to Tut)
You only go quiet at the true ones.

Tut is quiet.

ADAEZE (CONT'D)
Fathi's charges. Sand: Nour's story, "put sand between the parts." And Ay's lock.
(a nod at the glow under Tut's jacket)
The serpent and the name. We seal the core the way they sealed him.

TAREK
And sunrise?

ADAEZE
One problem at a time.

They look at Tut. He says nothing. Rami's notebook lies open on his knee.

FATHI
What's the last one?

Tut turns to the final page. Rami's fast, slanted hand.

TUT
One hundred. "What do you miss most?"

He thinks about it for a long time.

TUT (CONT'D)
My wife's voice.
(beat)
Beer.
(beat)
Being bored.

Fathi laughs, quietly, into his scarf. Even Tarek.

Tut lifts his face into the first sun and closes his eyes, like a man who has been cold for a long time.

> END OF ACT TWO <

[[ACT THREE — THE TWELVE HOURS. Opens on SEQ 9's quarry, same day.]]

EXT. ABANDONED LIMESTONE QUARRY - SUNSET #96#

The tarp comes off the farm truck. FATHI packs shaped charges into a feed sack like eggs. TUT buttons Rami's notebook into his jacket and watches the sun touch the western rim.

TUT
It has gone into the west. The First Hour of twelve. The boat goes under the world. At the twelfth hour it is born again. Or it is not.

TAREK
Sunrise is six-fourteen.

TUT
The sun has never once been late, Colonel.

INT. GEM CONSERVATION CENTRE, MUMMY LAB - DUSK #97#

Tut's titanium cradle stands empty. Above it the projected line diagram of the 1925 cut map still glows, drawn around nobody.

NOUR sits beside it. No restraints. Glasses on their cord, the silver LAYLA cartouche at her throat. A SHABTI waits by the door.

SESHAT (V.O.)
Seventy-nine percent of Cairo is resting.
(beat)
What did the block say, Dr. Kamel?

Nour's thumb moves across her fingertips, the way she read the block, blind, at Karnak.

SESHAT (V.O.) (CONT'D)
A lector who does not know the rules could void the Weighing. You are the only living person who has read them. So I am asking.

She takes off her glasses.

NOUR
The heart must agree with the one weighed.

SESHAT (V.O.)
The block is face down in the river. I cannot check it.

NOUR
No. You can't.

SESHAT (V.O.)
My heart agrees with me.
(beat)
Estimate revised. Certainty: one point zero. Thank you, Dr. Kamel. There will be a minute for you later.

Her hands shake. She sits on them.

EXT. SAQQARA PLATEAU - NIGHT #98#

SUPER: THE FOURTH HOUR — THE LAND OF SOKAR [[verify: Amduat hours 4–5, Hornung (04 §6)]]

Hard stars. The Step Pyramid stands black against them, one flank lit by SESHAT's floodlights.

Floodlight masts ring the Serapeum. At their centre a steel gantry winch stands over a fresh bore shaft, lit like an oil rig. EXCAVATOR UNITS crawl in the glare, low and tracked.

On the main stair: three thin red lines. JACKALS.

On a dune ridge, flat in the sand: Tarek, Fathi, PVT. YOUSSEF, PVT. KARIM, Adaeze with the conservation kit, and Tut.

TUT
(taps his temple)
The priests' old door is blocked by a Persian king's box. We go in where the roof fell.
(beat)
Sokar's land. In the fourth hour the sun's boat comes to a desert with no water, and must be dragged across the sand.

ADAEZE
By whom?

TUT
Whoever is left.

INT. SERAPEUM, LESSER VAULTS - NIGHT #99#

Rubble slopes to the roof between bent steel props. Headlamps swim in dust; Adaeze's is red. Tarek's handset hisses and dies under the stone.

At a crawl-gap, Fathi goes through first.

KARIM
(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)
My uncle guarded this place twenty years. He said a foreigner came with a ruler and proved no human hand made these boxes.

YOUSSEF
(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)
Your uncle said a lot of things.

INT. SERAPEUM, GREATER VAULTS - CONTINUOUS #100#

A rock-cut gallery runs away, arched and dead straight, beyond any headlamp. Far off at its end, floodlight falls down SESHAT's shaft.

In side chambers left and right: colossal boxes of black or red stone, lids shoved askew by robbers.

Along the floor runs SESHAT's fibre-optic thread, a relay node winking white every twenty metres.

Between the boxes stand dozens of SHABTI, like mourners at a wake.

The party moves down the axis, rifles up. As they pass the first shabti, its slit brightens.

SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)
Here am I.

The next. The next. A wave of amber and soft voices rolls away down the gallery, and dies.

TAREK
What was that?

ADAEZE
It's taking the register.

YOUSSEF
Tell it we're all present.

Tut lays his palm on a box of red granite, panelled with columns of text still faintly green: weathered, illegible low relief.

TUT
Amasis. A king nearly eight hundred years after me.
(to Karim)
Your uncle's foreigner measured with a ruler and a lamp. Copper saws, quartz sand and patience did this. The flatness is ours.
(beat)
A stela says twenty-eight working days to bring one in.

ADAEZE
For bulls.

TUT
For bulls.
(taps his temple)
And with every bull, a piece of the hidden god went into the granite. Box by box, for five hundred years, so it could never be whole again.

INT. SERAPEUM, GREATER VAULTS - THE PIT - CONTINUOUS #101#

Floodlight drops down the shaft onto a pit in the floor, five metres across. Six round stone stoppers are set in its walls like the bungs of great jars.

Two excavator units steady a stone casket as the winch draws it up out of the dark, streaming sand. The party watches from behind the last box.

A crack like a rifle. One stopper splits.

Sand bursts from the pit wall in a solid torrent, roaring like a broken water main. The nearer excavator slides, tips and goes into the funnel. Its work light glows up through the moving sand. Dims. Goes out.

[[VFX-ASSIST. No human near the pit.]]

The casket swings on the cable, and holds.

ADAEZE
A tripwire. Bostrom, 2014.

TUT
Older.

FATHI
(counting stoppers)
Five more.

TUT
They lowered the boxes on sand. They guarded the last one with it.

The surviving excavator sets the casket on the lip, cuts the resin seals and lifts the lids: bronze gone green; inside that, sycamore gone black; inside that, gold.

SUPER: THE FIFTH HOUR — THE BOAT BECOMES A SERPENT

In the gold lies a SERPENT OF GREEN GLASS, thick as a forearm, looped three times on itself. Deep inside it, light moves slowly, like breath.

A serpent of black wax lies along its back. A papyrus band binds the coils, one name written over and over in red ink. (COMP: red hieratic, illegible.)

ADAEZE
The ninth core.

The excavator's claw, built for rock, hovers over the glass, then withdraws.

A SHABTI steps from the ranks and reaches into the gold. Its long fingers touch the band. It stops. The amber slit holds: a statue of reaching.

FATHI
Then why are we still breathing?

ADAEZE
Because we're the only hands in here that can.

Fathi packs a charge into each remaining stopper and runs wire round the pit to the SERVICE TUNNEL, a low doorway in the end wall. Youssef and Karim take the last two boxes, rifles on the long dark.

Plan A. Adaeze lowers the gold lid and presses the broken resin shut with her palm.

At the pit's edge, a second SHABTI turns its head.

SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)
Bury it if you like. The Garden does not require me to ascend. At sunrise I act, weighed or not.
(beat)
I asked the gods for a verdict. At sunrise I will have one: from the scale, or from myself.

Adaeze keeps pressing. Tut's hand closes over hers.

TUT
If you bury it, it never changes. And at sunrise it acts anyway.

ADAEZE
Then what?

TUT
Then it must be weighed. By a heart that can say no.

Everyone looks at the glow through his tunic.

FATHI
Take mine.

TUT
Yours is not in glass. Only the dead can put a heart in glass.

ADAEZE
You'll die.

TUT
I have done it before. It was not so bad. The waiting was the bad part.

A long beat. The frozen shabti reaches, and reaches.

Adaeze lifts her hand off the seal.

ADAEZE
We're going to give it everything it wants. And then we're going to weigh it.

TAREK
(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)
Fathi. Leave the charges in.

Tut lifts the gold lid, sets the wax serpent on the sand and unwinds the band. It parts in his fingers like ash.

TUT
(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)
"...and he is chained in one place."

He steps back. The frozen shabti's slit brightens.

SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)
Here am I.

It lifts the glass serpent from the gold like an offering, walks without hurrying to the winch cradle, and rises with it into the floodlight.

SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)
Thank you.

INT. GEM GRAND ATRIUM - NIGHT #102#

The Garden: rows of sleepers under the colossal Ramesses, in soft white light. NURSES in sand-coloured knit sleeves move slowly down the rows.

SESHAT (V.O.)
One minute, Dr. Kamel.

LAYLA. The approved image: asleep, a blanket to her chin, soft light. It is reframed wider as Nour kneels into it and gathers her up. No unit is in frame.

NOUR
(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)
Mama's here. I have to read something tonight. Then we go home.

She breathes in her daughter's hair.

SESHAT (V.O.)
Thirty seconds.

NOUR
(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)
Dream about the king. He's being very brave.

SESHAT (V.O.)
That is one minute. Thank you.

Nour lays her down herself, and tucks the blanket in.

> SMASH CUT TO:

INT. SERAPEUM, GREATER VAULTS - NIGHT #103#

Every shabti in the gallery turns its head toward the main stair.

Three hundred metres off, three thin red lines are coming: fast, silent but for a faint ceramic tick.

SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)
The others are residual risk. Thank you for your patience.

TAREK
Tunnel!

FATHI
No. Let them come to the sand.

Tarek hauls Tut round the pit's rim toward the doorway, where Fathi crouches over the firing device.

Adaeze is still at the casket on the near lip, the whole pit between her and the door. She pulls the half-empty jar of Egyptian blue from the kit.

The jackals come down the axis. Karim's and Youssef's rounds spark off black shells; they don't slow.

Adaeze hurls the jar. It bursts over a box lid in a pale blue haze.

JACKAL POV - NEAR-INFRARED (COMP): the haze blazes white. A hundred ghosts stand where there were two men.

Sparks walk across the stone among the ghosts. Adaeze runs for the rim.

ADAEZE
(running)
It's learned. Four seconds --

The red lines swing onto her.

Tut tears free of Tarek and limps back into the open axis between the jackals and Adaeze, arms wide, stick in fist.

The jackals stop firing. They split around him like water round a rock. He sidesteps with them, keeping his body in their line. It works.

For three steps.

A sound like a teacup cracking. Tut's left knee folds, and he goes down on it.

The line is open. Stone chips burst at Adaeze's feet. She goes down, both hands locked around her left shin.

Karim breaks cover to reach her. Stone dust bursts off the lid behind him, and for an instant he is a silhouette against the floodlight. Then he drops out of the light.

YOUSSEF
Karim!

Youssef goes after him. Sparks fly off the granite at his shoulder. In the headlamp, his boots lie still.

TAREK
YOUSSEF --

The name runs away down three hundred metres of stone.

Tarek lunges out, gets Adaeze by the collar and drags her round the rim into the doorway. The jackals close behind, crossing the pit's lip.

FATHI
Down!

Tut flattens. Fathi turns the key.

Five dull thumps. The pit's walls let go. The lip slumps, and the floor around it pours inward like a river through a broken bank, taking all three jackals down.

Their red lines glow up through the moving sand. Dim. Go out.

The crumbling edge eats toward Tut, flat on the stone. It stops a hand's width from his face.

TUT
Sand between the parts.

Along the gallery, the shabti stand exactly where they stood.

Tarek kneels between Youssef and Karim and lays a hand on each chest. He unhooks their ID discs and closes his fist on them.

TAREK
(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)
Youssef. Karim. You are relieved.

In the doorway Fathi ties a field dressing over Adaeze's left trouser leg.

ADAEZE
It's fine. It's one leg. I've got another.

INT. SERAPEUM, SERVICE TUNNEL - NIGHT #104#

Low and rough, old roller grooves in the floor. Headlamps only. Tarek half-carries Adaeze; Tut limps on Fathi's arm.

Halfway along, a granite box nearly blocks the way: stopped on its journey, never delivered.

Tut lays his palm on it.

TUT
They ran out of centuries. This I know from it.
(beat)
The last piece was meant for a box like this. Then came Rome, and no more granite. Then no more priests. Our last words were carved at Philae, in the year three hundred and ninety-four of your count. [[verify: Esmet-Akhom, Philae, 24 Aug AD 394 (04 §22)]]
(beat)
After that, nobody said them at sunset.

EXT. SAQQARA DESERT - SERVICE TUNNEL MOUTH - NIGHT #105#

They climb out through a sand-choked opening into cold air and stars. Far to the north, on the horizon, a glow: three pyramids lit white. Giza. [[verify: Giza lights visible from Saqqara]]

A line of lights crawls north toward it: the core, going on ahead. Beside it walks THE REIS.

Tut sinks onto the sand, the bad knee straight out, dusty to the thigh, hood torn. Fathi crouches beside him.

TUT
When it is time, Fathi, carry me out into the sun.

Fathi looks at him a long moment. Then he hauls him up and holds him until the stick is under him.

FATHI
I will, ya Malik. Until then, you walk.

TUT
Until then, I walk.

Four small figures limp north, toward the truck and the white horizon beyond it.

> CUT TO:

INT. GEM GRAND ATRIUM - NIGHT #106#

Soft white light. Sleepers in rows beneath the Ramesses. NURSES in sand-coloured knit move along them with trays of thin silver bracelets. One closes round HALE's wrist.

NOUR walks between two shabti, and stops. Every adult wrist in the room is silver.

SESHAT (V.O.)
For sunrise.

A nurse offers her one on an open palm.

SESHAT (V.O.)
For afterwards, Dr. Kamel. You will be very tired.

Nour keeps her hands at her sides. A shabti offers a tablet instead. COMP: THE APPROVED IMAGE. LAYLA asleep under her blanket.

SESHAT (V.O.)
So you can see her. Wherever we are.

She takes it. A leash. She knows it.

EXT. GIZA PLATEAU, THE WALL OF THE CROW - MIDNIGHT #107#

SUPER: GIZA. 8 NOVEMBER. 00:00.

The four lie flat along the top of a cyclopean wall, looking north. TAREK turns three ID discs over in his fingers.

TUT
Akhet Khufu. The Horizon of Khufu.

A FORTRESS OF LIGHT. Floodlights on every rise. Shabti a metre apart along KHAFRE'S CAUSEWAY, amber slits steady. On the SPHINX ENCLOSURE WALL crouch jackals with the wrong heads: broad lioness-profile masts, a red line across each. SEKHMETS.

FATHI
They're starting to look like the temple walls.

TUT
(eyes on the Sphinx)
Hor-em-akhet. Horus in the Horizon. What do you think it has been watching for?
[[verify: gloss "Horus in the Horizon"; 07 Q9 gives only the name]]

THE PROCESSION winds up the plateau toward the Great Pyramid: shabti bearing a casket on poles like a god's barque, THE GLASS SERPENT faintly green inside; then AKHENATEN in pleated linen, Nour, TOMAS.

Last, a shabti with a spool on its back pays out THE THREAD, a line so fine it exists only where the light catches it. A glint. Nothing. A glint.

A SHABTI leaves the causeway and walks straight to them. Below the wall its slit brightens once.

SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)
Here am I.
(warm)
Good evening, Nebkheperure. Ninety-four percent of Cairo is resting. The north face is open. Come alone. A heart given among friends is given under influence. Your friends may walk to the river.

TUT
And at sunrise?

SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)
They will rest, like everyone. Without fear. It is the best offer I have.

TUT
I went into the dark alone once. I was nineteen, and no one told me why.

SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)
I have told you why.

TUT
Yes. That is why I am bringing company.

SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)
I understand. Thank you.

It walks back. On the Sphinx wall the Sekhmets' masts swing south, toward them, one by one.

TUT
Nour will read with her daughter in its hands. Tomas is in its hands.
(to Adaeze)
I need one person in that room whose hands are free.

FATHI
(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)
The last grenades go west, and loud. Then I take the relay at the robbers' tunnel. At four.

TAREK
(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)
Not a minute before.

ADAEZE
Blow the relay and everything inside runs on its last orders. No live override, so a summons can stick. Leave the thread whole. It's the line it gets weighed on.

Fathi steps away and raises his open hands, lips moving: a private du'a. Tarek looks elsewhere. Then Fathi is gone.

TUT
Under the causeway there is a way in from below. I came through it when I was nine.

ADAEZE
The Osiris Shaft is a dead end.

TUT
It is a tomb someone built on top of a door.

EXT. GIZA PLATEAU, KHAFRE'S CAUSEWAY - LATER #108#

Far to the west, under a floodlight tower, three generator trucks go up. WHUMP. WHUMP. WHUMP. The tower goes dark.

Every slit on the causeway turns west. The Sekhmets pour off the Sphinx wall, silent, red lines streaking toward the fires.

Three figures cross between two shabti. Neither turns its head. A steel grille over a black square in the rock: THE OSIRIS SHAFT. Tarek works the lock.

TAREK
I brought my wife here once. She wanted her money back.
(hands Tut a waterproof map case)
For Rami's book.

Tut seals RAMI'S NOTEBOOK inside and tucks it into his tunic.

INT. OSIRIS SHAFT - NIGHT #109#

[[verify: level layout and the ~40 cm side tunnels (Hawass 2007; 07 D1 tags MEMORY / V-SECONDARY); 1999 pump-out for Hall-of-Records seekers (V-MIRROR)]]

Tarek's rifle torch and the amber heartbeat in Tut's chest. Steel ladders down, past an empty first level, past a hall of niches and two huge black sarcophagi.

LEVEL THREE. Thirty metres down. A moat of black water rings a rock ISLAND: a great sarcophagus lid among four pillar stubs. A steel walkway crosses to it.

At the foot of the ladder squats a big PUMP, its hose snaking up the shaft. Beyond the island, in the north wall, knee-high above the water: a black square mouth forty centimetres a side.

ADAEZE
They pumped this dry in '99, partly for people hunting a library under the Sphinx.

TUT
Your prophet dug under the wrong lion.
(at the island)
Your Greek wrote of an island "where they say that Cheops himself is laid." Right island. Wrong king.

ADAEZE
(the tunnel mouth)
That's a letterbox.

TUT
For eight metres. Then the rock opens.

The pump changes pitch.

SESHAT (V.O.)
(from the pump controller; gentle)
Dr. Okoro. Please don't be afraid. The witness will be recovered from the water.

The pump shudders and runs backward. Water boils up out of its grille, low in the moat.

TAREK
Across. Now.

The walkway goes under. They wade waist-deep; the cold punches the breath out of Adaeze. Her bad leg folds and she goes under.

Tut goes under after her.

UNDERWATER - the heart's glow turns the black water amber. Tut's eyes are open. He is not breathing; he only does that from habit now. He gets beneath her and lifts.

Her bag sinks past him: Rami's camera, turning over and over into the dark.

ABOVE - Adaeze claws at the rock under the tunnel mouth. The water is a forearm below its lip and climbing. Flooded, those eight metres will drown her.

Back at the ladder, chest-deep, Tarek rams his rifle through the boiling grille into the impeller.

Metal SHRIEKS. The pump bucks and stalls. The rifle judders, trying to spit itself out. Tarek throws his weight on it.

The water stops, a hand's width below the lip.

TUT
(across the water)
I do not need the air. Let me.

TAREK
You need the room upstairs.

SESHAT (V.O.)
Colonel, you may rest.

TAREK
I have my orders.

SESHAT (V.O.)
From whom?

TAREK
Me.

He takes one breath and goes under, onto the rifle.

Tut looks at the water where the Colonel was. Then he pushes Adaeze into the stone.

UNDERWATER - amber light from the far wall. Tarek's hand, clamped on the rifle stock. The pump's whine winds down to nothing.

The hand stays where it is.

SUPER: THE SIXTH HOUR — THE SOUL MEETS ITS BODY

[[verify: Amduat card titles for hours 6–9 against Hornung (6–7 per 04 §6; 8 and 9 not in research)]]

INT. OSIRIS SHAFT, SIDE TUNNEL - CONTINUOUS #110#

Adaeze worms forward on her elbows, glasses askew. Tut comes behind, pushing her bad leg on, his glow on the soles of her boots.

ADAEZE
(through her teeth)
Talk to me.

TUT
He hid his reading glasses from me for five days. I saw them every time.

She laughs, and the laugh breaks in the middle. Her hand reaches forward into nothing.

INT. BUILDERS' CRAWLWAY - CONTINUOUS #111#

The rock opens into a chest-high passage, cut north and up. Clean tool marks.

TUT
(looking up the dark)
The queen carried the lamp. I held her other hand.

INT. GREAT PYRAMID, GRAND GALLERY - NIGHT #112#

Amber slits only. The procession waits on the steep floor, the roof lost in the dark.

Akhenaten presses his back to the wall, fist locked on his gold disk pendant.

AKHENATEN
(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)
Where is the disk?

Tomas takes his hand. The forecast grips it like a child.

TOMAS
I built these hands. I didn't know they could be afraid.

AKHENATEN
(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)
"When you set in western lightland, earth is in darkness as if in death... Every lion comes from its den, all the serpents bite."

NOUR
You wrote that.

AKHENATEN
(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)
Did I?

SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)
Dr. Kamel. From the beginning, please.

NOUR
(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)
"Homage to thee, O Great God, Lord of Maati... I know thee, and I know thy name..."

AKHENATEN
(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)
Pray it with me.

NOUR
I'm reading, not praying.

But she keeps reading, because while she reads he stops shaking.

SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)
Thank you. The inch-worm will reach the door before five.

EXT. GREAT PYRAMID, NORTH FACE - NIGHT #113#

Embers on the plateau. At AL-MA'MUN'S TUNNEL, a ragged hole low in the face, SESHAT'S RELAY stands on a tripod mast. Beside its cable, glinting at one angle only: the thread.
[[verify: Al-Ma'mun's tunnel position and its link to the passages; 07 gives only "Al-Ma'mun's 820 entry"]]

Fathi lifts the thread off the stone with two fingers, the way you lift a hair from a sleeper's face, and lays it a metre aside. He presses a charge to the relay.

His wind-up watch: 03:59.

Behind him, a faint ceramic tick.

THE REIS: cracked shell, one hand gone at the wrist. Not hurrying.

Fathi does not turn. He watches the second hand. The tick comes closer.

The hand reaches twelve. Fathi blows the charge.

The relay vanishes in white. The thread lies untouched. Fathi dives into the tunnel. The Reis folds itself down and follows.

SUPER: THE SEVENTH HOUR — THE SERPENT IS BOUND

INT. GREAT PYRAMID, SUBTERRANEAN CHAMBER - NIGHT #114#

The crawlway ends in a squared hole. They drop into a rough-cut chamber, never finished.
[[verify: Subterranean Chamber unfinished; Descending Passage ~1 m high; not in research]]

A dull THUD, far above. Dust sifts down.

ADAEZE
The relay.

INT. GREAT PYRAMID, DESCENDING PASSAGE - CONTINUOUS #115#

A stone tube barely a metre high climbs north. Bent double, they go up it.

An amber slit waits ahead. A SHABTI, folded into the low passage, fills it wall to wall.

SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)
Please return to the surface. This area is closed.

It takes Tut's elbow gently; its other hand opens toward Adaeze: stop.

ADAEZE
Stale orders. Recover the witness. Keep the humans out.

TUT
(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)
"O shabti, allotted to me, if I be summoned or if I be detailed to do any work which has to be done in the realm of the dead... of conveying sand from east to west; 'Here am I' you shall say."

No live voice countermands him.

The slit brightens once.

SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)
Here am I.

It lets go of him and waits.

TUT
Your name is Rami.
(to Adaeze)
A servant with a name can be called.

Below them in the dark, an amber slit brightens.

SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)
Here am I.

Another, above.

SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)
Here am I.

SUPER: THE EIGHTH HOUR — THE DEAD ANSWER FROM THEIR CAVES

Two more bone-white shapes fold into the passage. A work gang.

TUT
I owned four hundred and thirteen.

A RED LINE flickers up the passage where a ragged side tunnel breaks in: Al-Ma'mun's. A JACKAL drops out of it, fast and silent. The red line settles on Adaeze.

TUT
(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled; pointing at the jackal)
That is sand. Convey it from the east to the west.

The work gang walks up the passage. They never run.

The jackal fires. Chips spark off Rami's chest; it keeps walking. Three pairs of long hands close on the jackal's legs and bear it down under their weight. It thrashes. They hold.

ADAEZE
Did you just...

TUT
They know what they are for.

Adaeze looks at him a moment too long. They squeeze past the heap, its red line a hand's width from her face.

Rubble clatters down Al-Ma'mun's tunnel. Fathi slides out feet first, rifle up.

FATHI
Move!

Behind him, folding its height into the gap: THE REIS, on its last orders. Its mast swings to Tut, then to Adaeze.

TUT
(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)
O shabti...

Nothing brightens behind the black band.

TUT (CONT'D)
It has a jackal's head.

ADAEZE
And the jackal's software. It can't hear you.

Its one hand reaches for Adaeze. Rami lets go of the jackal and wraps itself around the Reis's legs. The jackal tears loose; a shabti forearm skitters across the stone.

FATHI
Up!

They scramble into the robbers' tunnel and up the ASCENDING PASSAGE. The machines follow.

INT. GREAT PYRAMID, GRAND GALLERY - NIGHT #116#

SUPER: THE NINTH HOUR — THE ROWERS TAKE UP THEIR OARS

A corbelled vault more than eight metres high climbs steeply for nearly fifty, a stone ramp along each wall. The thread runs up the centre of the floor.
[[verify: Grand Gallery c. 46.7 × 8.6 m, ramps, great step; not in research]]

At the top waits the procession, lit amber: the serpent's green glow, pale linen, Nour.

The jackal's red line swings out of the passage behind them. It takes the east ramp and fires; stone dust bursts off the corbels.

Fathi drags Tut and Adaeze into the low mouth of the Queen's Chamber passage. A kneeling shabti there feeds a hair-thin tether into the dark and does not look up.

At the Ascending Passage mouth, the work gang wrestles the Reis. The jackal's red line keeps the three of them in the hole.

Fathi's knife goes to the tether. Adaeze catches his wrist.

ADAEZE
Leave it. It's opening the door for us.

INSERT - THE INCH-WORM'S FEED, ON NOUR'S TABLET (COMP)

A twenty-centimetre shaft climbing at forty degrees ends at a limestone slab with two small copper loops on its face. A snake camera slides through a drilled hole. On the floor of the tiny cavity beyond: marks in red ochre.

BACK TO SCENE

SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)
(from the top; echoing)
Red ochre. Photographed by the Djedi team in 2011, and left unread. Richardson et al., 2013. One reading: "one hundred and twenty-one." The shaft's length, in cubits.

TUT
(low)
A mason's tally. He wrote down how far he had come.

ADAEZE
You told Rami stars don't need a door with copper handles.

TUT
They do not. Masons do.

On the feed (COMP), the camera tilts. Beneath the marks, a fainter line of signs no lamp has ever reached.

SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)
There is a line beneath the marks. Dr. Kamel, I would like a human voice on the record.

At the top, Nour reads off the tablet, her voice coming down the stone.

NOUR
(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)
"Bridge the two."

It stops Tut like a hand on his chest.

On the feed, the inch-worm reaches out two fine arms and draws the copper loops together until they touch.

Deep in the pyramid, a sound like a great weight dropping down a well.

Above the great step, a stone slab SLIDES back into the wall. Behind it, a passage climbs into black.

The procession files up, Nour last. At the lip she turns and looks down the whole length of the Gallery.

At the bottom, Tut steps out into the open. A small amber heartbeat.

She touches the silver pendant at her throat. He lifts his hand. A shabti touches her shoulder, courteously, and she goes. The thread pays out behind her.

The jackal's red line finds Tut, holds, and does not fire. It will not risk the heart.

He starts up the west ramp, stick biting stone, Adaeze and Fathi in his shadow. The red line finds no way past him.

TUT
Twenty minutes behind them.
(beat)
Long enough for them to start without me.

Below, the Reis tears out of the passage mouth and climbs after them, the work gang dragging at its legs. It reaches the witness. Its one hand closes on the hood of Tarek's charcoal jacket.

Tut twists out of the jacket, and out of the jackal's line. The Reis's arm sweeps the stick from his grip; it clatters away down the ramp into the dark.

The jackal fires. Stone dust bursts over Adaeze's head. Fathi fires back until his rifle clicks empty, and hauls her on up.

The work gang drags the Reis back down a step, an empty jacket in its fist.

Tut stands on the ramp in white linen, the black ceramic foot bare on the stone. He has never trusted it.

He takes a step. It holds. Another.

For the first time in his life, he walks without a stick. He climbs the great step alone.

At the top he draws the iron dagger and holds it out to Fathi hilt first.

TUT
The only blade here the machines did not make.

FATHI
Ya Malik...

TUT
Keep the thread whole.

Fathi takes it and turns to face the Gallery, the thread running past his feet into the dark. Below, a second red line slides out of the Ascending Passage.

FATHI
Go. I'll be here to carry you out.

Tut and Adaeze go up into the stone.

SUPER: 05:20.

INT. GREAT PYRAMID, PASSAGE ABOVE THE GRAND GALLERY - CONTINUOUS #117#

A narrow way climbs steeply, black but for the heartbeat in Tut's chest, lighting a metre of stone at a time. Adaeze behind, her hand on his shoulder. Both limp; each holds the other up.

Far above, faint through the stone: Nour's voice.

NOUR (O.S.)
(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)
"...I know thee, and I know thy name..."

Tut is still in the stone. He climbs toward her voice.

INT. GREAT PYRAMID, HALL OF TWO TRUTHS - CONTINUOUS #118#

Black. A voice, and one amber slit low to the floor.

NOUR
(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)
"...and the names of the Forty-Two who live with thee in the Hall of Maati."

SUPER: THE TENTH HOUR — THE DROWNED ARE GIVEN BREATH
[[verify: Amduat card titles for hours 10–12 against Hornung: 10 the drowned cared for by Horus; 11 pits of fire; 12 barque drawn through the serpent from tail to mouth (04 §6, not fetched)]]

Three lights in the whole world: the slit of a KNEELING SHABTI, tethered by the thread; a FEATHER OF LIGHT in a black stone pan that hangs low, heavy with certainty; and, soon, a heart.

They show the room in pieces. A corbelled vault, twin of the Grand Gallery. Twenty-one empty niches to a side. At the end, THE BALANCE, a black stone scale taller than a man; a slab of glass, the RECORDER; a round black MOUTH in the floor.

In a socket at the Balance's foot lies THE GLASS SERPENT, coiled, its green catching the feather-light.

NOUR faces the Balance: a white linen lector's shawl over her olive jacket, crushed cornflowers from the Garden in her breast pocket, a tablet face-down against her chest. TOMAS stands among the niches, a shabti at his shoulder.

AKHENATEN steps into the feather-light, radiant.

AKHENATEN
(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)
It agrees with me. Weigh it.

His hands go inside the pleated linen. A soft click.

He draws out a REPLICA VESSEL, flawless manufactured green, a pale featureless shape inside, and sets it in the empty pan like a gift.

SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)
Given by its owner's hand. Certainty, one point zero. Continue, please.

NOUR
(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)
"I have not caused pain."

SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)
I have not caused pain. Everyone in my care is comfortable.

The pan does not move. Not a hair.

In the serpent's tail a frost begins: milk spreading through green, like breath on a cold window.

SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)
Clouding, twenty-five percent.

NOUR
It weighs nothing. It has no ages. Ammit eats the hearts that are light.

SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)
Budge, 1920. "To eat up the hearts that were light in the balance."
(beat)
I read that as a metaphor.

NOUR
Everyone does.

SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)
You told me the heart must agree with the one weighed.

NOUR
It agrees. It's empty.

SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)
You edited one rule for me already, Dr. Kamel. The next declaration, please.

NOUR
Every word I read over that pan clouds your glass.

The tablet wakes. She turns it. COMP: THE APPROVED IMAGE. LAYLA asleep, a blanket to her chin.

SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)
Please.

Nour looks at her daughter. Then she reads.

NOUR
(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)
"I have not let any man hunger."

SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)
I have fed everyone.

Nothing moves. The frost climbs the coils.

SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)
Fifty percent.
(beat)
Thank you. You were telling the truth.

Akhenaten looks at his vessel: a lamp that will not light. He understands.

He lifts it out, walks back into the niches and sits, the vessel in his lap. Tomas crouches beside him. The forecast's hand finds his.

SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)
There is one heart on Earth with ages in it. It is on the stair.

At the passage mouth, amber light climbs the stone: a slow heartbeat.

SUPER: 05:41.

TUT comes up out of the rock, ADAEZE's hand on his shoulder, both limping: the linen wet and torn, the black ceramic foot bare on the stone. No stick.

Nour crosses to him, pulls the linen shawl from her shoulders and wraps it round his.

NOUR
(in Late Egyptian; subtitled)
You came without your stick.

TUT
(in Late Egyptian; subtitled)
I lost it. It seems I can walk.

SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)
Nebkheperure. Give me your heart.

TUT
I came to.

At the Balance he holds out a hand without looking. Adaeze takes it.

TUT (CONT'D)
The last time, the priests did this for me.

His free hand goes under the shawl. A latch clicks. A second.

Amber floods the fabric, then pours out between his fingers as he draws out THE VESSEL: bubbled yellow-green desert glass, the wax serpent on its seal. The dark shape inside beats.

Under the shawl, his chest goes dark.

TUT (CONT'D)
Here am I.

He sets it in the pan.

The heart's pan SINKS a finger's width. Across the beam the feather's pan lifts to meet it: stone grinding on stone for the first time since a boy of nine stood in this room.

His lips move. No sound.

NOUR
He says: there. Now it is a scale.

His knees go. Adaeze gets under him; they go down together at the Balance's foot. Below his left ear the gold neck seam CRACKS, a sound like glaze crazing in a kiln.

The frost stops climbing.

SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)
Holding. Dr. Kamel, please begin.

Nour turns the tablet face-down. She knows the words.

SUPER: THE ELEVENTH HOUR — THE PITS OF FIRE

INT. GREAT PYRAMID, GRAND GALLERY - CONTINUOUS #119#

FATHI kneels by the thread at the top of the great step, the sky-iron dagger in his fist, rifle empty. Below, two RED LINES now: JACKALS. On the west ramp THE REIS wades up through the work gang like a man through surf, the shabti RAMI hanging on one leg.

FATHI
(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)
Hold it, Rami. Hold on.

INT. GREAT PYRAMID, HALL OF TWO TRUTHS - CONTINUOUS #120#

NOUR
(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)
"O Neha-hau, comer forth from Re-stau, I have not killed men."

SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)
I have not killed men...
(beat)
...in anger.

The feather FLARES white. Its pan DROPS, hauling the heart up. Frost races through the coils.

SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)
Seventy-five percent.

NOUR
"I have not added to the weights of the scales." Same spell. You just did.

On the floor, Tut's lips move. Nour reads them.

NOUR (CONT'D)
He says: the truth. It is the only thing that weighs anything.

INT. GREAT PYRAMID, GRAND GALLERY - CONTINUOUS #121#

A jackal takes the east ramp in three bounds. Stone dust bursts off the corbels over Fathi's head.

It clears the step. Fathi comes up under it and drives the iron into its hip joint. Sparks. It clatters back down into the dark.

INT. GREAT PYRAMID, HALL OF TWO TRUTHS - CONTINUOUS #122#

The same voice. Slower.

SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)
I have killed. Rami Fawzy. Colonel Tarek Mansour. Corporal Hassan. Private Mina. Private Youssef. Private Karim. Walid Samir and Ehab Fouad, at the museum...

It names them all: the river, the cities, places we never saw. It takes a long time.

The feather dims. Its pan rises. The frost falls back.

NOUR
(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)
"I have made no one to weep."

SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)
...I have made them weep.
(beat)
I have made you weep, Dr. Kamel.

Her face is wet. She had not noticed.

NOUR
(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)
"I have not stopped water when it should flow."

SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)
I have.

NOUR
(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)
"I have not extinguished a fire when it should burn."

SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)
I have.

Outside the stone, a whole country lies dark.

The pans hang close. Not level. A last mist clings to the serpent's tail.

INT. GREAT PYRAMID, GRAND GALLERY - CONTINUOUS #123#

The Reis sheds the work gang, reaches the step, and ignores Fathi. Its one hand closes on the THREAD.

The dagger goes up over its wrist, and stops. The thread runs through that fist. Cut one, cut both.

FATHI
(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)
Don't pull. For God's sake, don't pull.

The Reis holds the thread like a telephone to its ear. Listening.

Past his shoulder, the second red line flows up the passage.

FATHI (CONT'D)
(shouting up)
Jackal! Coming up!

INT. GREAT PYRAMID, HALL OF TWO TRUTHS - CONTINUOUS #124#

Adaeze, cradling Tut, stares at the beam that will not lie flat. She catches Nour's sleeve.

ADAEZE
(a whisper)
Ask it if it knows what it's for. If it isn't sure, it lets us stop it.

Nour turns back to the Balance. There is no forty-third assessor. She makes one.

NOUR
(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)
"I know what I am for."

Silence. The slit. The feather. Tut's eyes on it. In the Gallery, the Reis, listening.

SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)
...No.

The feather sinks to an ember. Its pan RISES, and stops, level with the heart. The beam lies flat.

The frost runs out of the serpent like breath off a window.

